const EXPLANATIONS = {"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Annual influenza virus vaccination is recommended for all patients greater than 6 months of age. Inactivated influenza virus vaccine can be safely administered via intramuscular injection to patients with HIV, who may be at higher risk for severe illness and influenza-related complications. While not specifically recommended prior to travel, eligible patients should receive the influenza vaccination during the autumn months prior to the typical influenza season.</p><p>Influenza is a respiratory viral illness that can be complicated by lower respiratory tract infections and/or bacterial superinfections, especially in elderly or immunocompromised patients. Symptoms include fever, chills, cough, myalgias, malaise, headaches, nausea, and vomiting. The Centers for Disease Control recommends annual influenza virus vaccination for all patients greater than 6 months of age. The influenza vaccine is available as a trivalent or quadrivalent intramuscular injection of inactivated virus or as a nasal spray of live attenuated virus. Inactivated influenza virus vaccine can be safely administered via intramuscular injection to patients with HIV, who may be at a higher risk for severe illness and influenza-related complications. Influenza is a seasonal illness, typically affecting patients from the late fall to early spring months. This patient is travelling during the autumn months and should receive the vaccination for influenza virus.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Human papillomavirus (HPV) (Choice A) is sexually transmitted and is associated with genital warts along with premalignant and malignant lesions of the cervical, anogenital, and oropharyngeal regions. HPV vaccines have significantly decreased the incidence and prevalence of HPV and protect against HPV-related malignancies such as cervical and anal squamous cell carcinoma. The vaccine should ideally be administered before the teenage years because the immune response is most robust, and the onset of sexual activity may occur during the teenage years. HPV vaccine is not specifically recommended prior to travel.</p><p>Measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) virus (Choice C) vaccine is a live attenuated vaccine that involves two doses: one at 12 to 15 months and one between 4 to 6 years of age. While the MMR vaccine can be safely given to unvaccinated HIV-positive patients with CD4+ cell counts &gt;200/mm³, boosters of MMR vaccine are not usually necessary in patients with HIV or prior to international travel.</p><p>Meningococcal (Choice D) vaccine is a conjugate vaccine that is indicated for all children, with an additional serogroup B vaccine indicated for all children over 10 years of age at increased risk for meningococcal disease (eg, dormitory, military living). Meningococcal vaccine is not necessary prior to international travel.</p><p>Rabies (Choice E) vaccine, which is available as a live attenuated or inactivated vaccine, may be advisable for travelers visiting particular high-risk areas (eg, parts of Africa or southern Asia) but is not routinely required for all international travelers. Rabies may be transmitted in developing countries through animal bites, most commonly by dogs. Preexposure prophylaxis with rabies vaccine is administered in a series of three intramuscular injections. Patients with HIV can safely receive inactivated rabies vaccine.</p><p>No vaccine (Choice F) is incorrect. Patients with HIV can safely receive inactivated influenza vaccine.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Diminished thermoregulatory capacity caused by increased age results in impaired thirst, blood vessel dilation, and reduced ability to sweat. Decreased ability to dissipate heat results in an increased risk for heat stroke.</p><p>Heat stroke is characterized by an increased body temperature that occurs when the body is exposed to high environmental temperatures and high humidity. Excessive heat exposure and increased core body temperature lead to cardiovascular and neurologic dysfunction with secondary tachycardia, tachypnea, and hypotension. Vasodilation of superficial blood vessels may lead to multi-organ system failure, including hepatic and renal ischemia, rhabdomyolysis, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Treatment includes cooling of the patient with fans or ice water, continuous core temperature monitoring, and support of airways, breathing, and circulation. Heat stroke generally requires neurologic symptoms to be diagnosed, whereas heat exhaustion presents similarly but with normal neurologic examination. Heat stroke commonly occurs in young athletes or in elderly patients with impaired thermoregulatory capacity, as evidenced by impaired thirst, blood vessel dilation, and reduced ability to sweat, all reducing the ability to dissipate heat.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, F, G, I, and J.</p><p>Decreased airway elasticity (Choice A) and increased lung compliance (Choice I) related to increased age subsequently lead to the loss of alveolar surface area caused by collapse of alveoli and fewer areas for gas exchange. This can lead to symptoms like emphysema and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Normal baroreflex activation involves changes to the heart rate in response to blood pressure. Normally, decreased blood pressure would lead to an increase in heart rate to restore mean arterial pressure. In the setting of an age-related decrease in baroreflex sensitivity (Choice B), heart rate does not increase as expected in response to decreased blood pressure, which can cause syncope, lightheadedness, and altered mental status. This patient is tachycardic, making this scenario less likely.</p><p>Decreased mucociliary clearance (Choice C) can lead to an increased risk for aspiration pneumonia. This patient's increased body temperature is likely secondary to external sources, and clear lungs argues against pneumonia as a source of her presentation.</p><p>Decreased thyroid function (Choice D) leading to hypothyroidism would present with signs and symptoms such as weight gain, lethargy, fatigue, constipation, hair loss, and cold intolerance.</p><p>Diastolic cardiac dysfunction (Choice E) would present with signs of heart failure including volume overload, pulmonary edema, lower extremity edema, and ascites.</p><p>Impaired cardiac response to exercise (Choice F) would lead to decreased exercise tolerance with symptoms such as dyspnea on exertion, lightheadedness, and syncope.</p><p>Impaired T-lymphocyte function (Choice G) would result in impaired cellular immunity, which can make a patient susceptible to infections. This patient's fever is likely secondary to external sources, rather than an infection.</p><p>Renal salt wasting (Choice J) can occur in the setting of 21-hydroxylase deficiency leading to cortisol and aldosterone deficiency. These patients also typically present with alkalosis, hyperkalemia, and hypovolemia.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Deficiency of the terminal complement proteins prevents the formation of the MAC, which is a critical component in the complement cascade leading to formation of a pore in the membrane of pathogenic organisms. Deficiency predisposes to recurrent infections with Neisseria species.</p><p>Deficiency of terminal components of complement is the most likely cause of this patient's recent and recurrent infection with Neisseria meningitidis. The presence of fever, nuchal rigidity, and cerebrospinal fluid analysis demonstrating gram-negative diplococci is diagnostic for bacterial meningitis. Deficiency of terminal complement factors (eg, C5-C9) impairs the formation of the membrane attack complex (MAC) and predisposes patients to infection with Neisseria species, although infections tend to occur with uncommon serotypes. While the complement cascade can be activated via the classical, lectin, or alternative pathways, each pathway converges on the same point whereby C3 is cleaved to release C3a and C3b. The C3 convertase complex subsequently leads to activation of C5, with formation of CS5a and C5b, the latter of which complexes with C6, C7, C8, and C9 to form the MAC. The MAC creates a transmembrane pore that interferes with the integrity of the cellular membrane, leading to cell lysis. Screening for terminal complement deficiency usually starts with determination of the CH50, a diagnostic test that measures the ability of the patient's serum to lyse sheep erythrocytes coated with antibodies directed against erythrocyte membrane proteins. Because this test measures the activity of factors C1-C9, a deficiency at any point will lead to an abnormal result, although in patients with terminal complement deficiency, the CH50 concentration will be very decreased or even zero. In addition to treating infections, further treatment should include vaccination against N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, and S. pneumoniae.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Common variable immunodeficiency (Choice A) classically presents in the second to fourth decades of life as recurrent sinopulmonary infections but does not predispose patients to infection with N. meningitidis. Diagnosis is made by measuring serum immunoglobulin concentrations, which are decreased.</p><p>Impaired cell-mediated immunity (Choice C) may refer to a broad range of conditions, from acquired conditions leading to lymphocyte dysfunction such as HIV to genetic conditions such as leukocyte adhesion deficiency, chronic granulomatous disease, and many others. Medications, including chemotherapy drugs and monoclonal antibodies targeting specific populations of immune cells, may also lead to impaired cell-mediated immunity. The findings are as diverse as the conditions that lead to immunodeficiency, but none specifically predispose patients to recurrent Neisseria infections.</p><p>Leukocyte adhesion deficiency (Choice D) is usually characterized by recurrent bacterial infections and delayed detachment of the umbilical cord. Patients have normal concentrations of neutrophils, but the cells are unable to effectively migrate to sites of infection.</p><p>Phagocytic cell oxidative metabolism deficiency (Choice E) describes chronic granulomatous disease. Deficiency in NADPH oxidase inhibits the respiratory burst within phagocytic cells such as neutrophils thereby leading to an increased susceptibility to catalase positive organisms. While N. meningitidis is catalase positive, recurrent infections with this organism in isolation would be unusual for this disease.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute retroviral syndrome refers to acute infection with HIV and commonly presents with diffuse lymphadenopathy, diarrhea, sore throat, malaise, fever, and weight loss. Patients may also present with opportunistic infections or may be entirely asymptomatic. Diagnosis in patients who do not meet classical risk criteria requires a high degree of clinical suspicion. Testing with a fourth-generation antigen/antibody test in addition to assessment of the HIV viral load is requisite.</p><p>Measurement of plasma HIV viral load is the most appropriate next step in management of this patient with high-risk sexual behavior and with signs and symptoms of acute retroviral syndrome. The presentation of primary HIV infection can vary significantly between patients; some may experience only a mild febrile illness while others experience a severe, mononucleosis-like syndrome. The time between infection and onset of symptoms is variable, with incubation time lasting several weeks or more. During acute infection, there is a high degree of viral replication followed by widespread infection of CD4+ T-lymphocytes. While the T-lymphocyte count may drop transiently, it is often normal in early HIV unless patients present with signs of opportunistic infection. Initial laboratory evaluation includes the use of a fourth-generation HIV test, which tests for HIV p24 antigen and antibodies, combined with direct measurement of the HIV viral load using reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction. It is common in early infection for patients to have a negative fourth-generation test but a high viral load; this is known as the window period. Once the diagnosis is made, labs should be sent for mutational analysis and genotyping, but this should not delay the immediate institution of treatment with antiretroviral therapy. Therapy typically includes a combination of at least three different drugs from at least two different antiretroviral classes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Serology for herpes simplex virus (HSV) (Choice B) is rarely helpful. While acute manifestations of HSV may range from oral and genital lesions to cases of fulminant liver failure, encephalitis, and meningitis, the role of serology is limited because patients with acute infection may not possess antibodies to HSV. Viral culture or PCR for detection of HSV DNA from specific tissues is more useful. Additionally, this patient's symptoms do not fit with a diagnosis of disseminated HSV.</p><p>Throat swab culture for influenza (Choice C) is incorrect. Current assays to detect influenza utilize PCR, not viral culture. Additionally, these swabs are typically obtained from the nasopharynx, not the throat.</p><p>Acyclovir therapy (Choice D) would be appropriate for patients with evidence of HSV infection, which most commonly manifests as oral and genital lesions, but may include liver failure, encephalitis, meningitis, or ocular symptoms as well. Prior to prescribing acyclovir, the diagnosis of HSV should be highly suspected if not confirmed.</p><p>Azithromycin therapy (Choice E), often in conjunction with a penicillin or cephalosporin antibiotic, is indicated for the treatment of community-acquired pneumonia, which is diagnosed in patients with fever, cough, and an infiltrate on chest x-ray. This patient has symptoms that are not easily explained by bacterial pneumonia. Azithromycin is also used in the management of sexually transmitted infections.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: CML results from constitutive activation of ABL kinase, which causes unregulated cellular proliferation of multiple lineages of white blood cells. Splenomegaly, thrombocytosis, and peripheral leukocytosis are classic features.</p><p>Chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML) is the most likely diagnosis in this asymptomatic male with splenomegaly, thrombocytosis, and leukocytosis with a white blood cell (WBC) differential demonstrating cells of different lineages. CML is diagnosed by the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome, a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22, which causes constitutive activation of the ABL kinase protein. Activation of ABL kinase leads to subsequent activation of the JAK/STAT and Ras/MAPK/ERK pathways, leading to increased hematopoietic proliferation, inhibition of normal checkpoint inhibition, and resistance to apoptosis. Typical laboratory findings in CML include leukocytosis with an increase in nearly all cell lines. Basophilia and eosinophilia are particularly characteristic. Splenomegaly, which at times can be massive, is common. Leukostasis, a condition in which leukocytosis leads to end organ damage from occlusion of capillaries by malignant cells, is uncommon in CML. This contrasts with conditions such as acute leukemias in which circulating blasts, which are large and not particularly pliable, easily lodge in capillaries causing pulmonary and neurologic complications. Treatment of CML is with tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKI) such as imatinib or dasatinib. Such medications are effective and often result in complete molecular response (remission) within several months of initiation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (Choice A) is a malignancy of mature, differentiated B lymphocytes. Common findings include lymphocytosis with peripheral smear showing lymphocytes with large blue nuclei and scant cytoplasm, many of which may appear ruptured (known as smudge cells). Treatment with ibrutinib is initiated if patients have profound cytopenias or disabling symptoms such as malaise and bulky lymphadenopathy.</p><p>Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML) (Choice C) is a myeloproliferative neoplasm of monocytes and presents with an increased peripheral monocyte count. Myelocytes and monocytes are both derived from undifferentiated multipotent stem cells, but they are distinctly different cell lines. This patient's peripheral blood smear does not show an increased circulating concentration of monocytes.</p><p>Plasma cell myeloma (Choice D), known as multiple myeloma, is a disease of abnormal plasma cells and classically presents with hypercalcemia, renal disease, anemia, bone fractures, and infections. Malignant plasma cells secrete large quantities of monoclonal immunoglobulin, which can be detected with the use of serum protein electrophoresis. The malignant plasma cells primarily reside in the bone marrow and cause cytopenias through the displacement of other normal progenitor cells. As such, peripheral leukocytosis is not typical of this condition.</p><p>Reactive leukocytosis (Choice E) is often seen in response to infection, trauma, or acute stressors such as acute coronary syndrome. In patients with this condition, peripheral blood smear discloses leukocytosis with neutrophilia often exceeding 80% of the total cell count, and in the case of severe infection may show an increased percentage of immature neutrophils. This patient's differential and asymptomatic presentation are not consistent with reactive leukocytosis.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Anemia of chronic disease is a common finding in autoimmune and inflammatory disease, chronic infections, and malignancy. Increased hepcidin concentration decrease iron absorption and increase iron storage in the bone marrow, preventing use of iron by erythrocyte precursors, thereby resulting in anemia. Therapy should focus on treating the underlying disease state.</p><p>No further evaluation or treatment is indicated at this time for this patient with rheumatoid arthritis, microcytic anemia, and iron studies disclosing increased ferritin concentration. Patients with microcytic anemia should be evaluated for iron deficiency through measurement of the total serum iron concentration, ferritin, and the total iron-binding capacity (TIBC). Iron deficiency anemia (IDA) manifests as decreased serum iron concentration, increased TIBC, and decreased ferritin. Calculation of the transferrin saturation is also useful. This is calculated by dividing the serum iron concentration by the total iron-binding capacity, then multiplying by 100. Numbers less than 20% indicate iron deficiency while numbers between 20% and 50% are considered normal. This patient has an increased ferritin concentration and a transferrin saturation of 20%, both of which are more consistent with anemia of chronic disease (ACD). ACD is common in patients with autoimmune or inflammatory, infectious, or malignant conditions. The pathophysiology may relate to upregulated hepcidin, resulting in reduced intestinal absorption of iron and reduced release of iron from macrophages. While patients may not be iron deficient, their iron stores are unavailable for hematopoiesis, leading to anemia. Treatment focuses on the underlying condition, which in this case is rheumatoid arthritis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Oral iron therapy (Choice A) given either daily or every other day is indicated for patients with evidence of IDA, which presents with microcytic anemia, decreased serum iron concentration, increased TIBC, and transferrin saturation less than 20%.</p><p>Subcutaneous erythropoietin (EPO) therapy (Choice B) is often given to patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD). EPO is a glycoprotein primarily secreted by interstitial cells in the kidney and functions to stimulate erythropoiesis. Patients with ESRD have decreased concentration of EPO and subsequently develop anemia. Subcutaneous injections of EPO are indicated for patients with ESRD who have hemoglobin concentration below 10 g/dL after other causes of anemia, including iron deficiency, have been ruled out.</p><p>Transfusion of packed red blood cells (Choice C) is usually indicated when the hemoglobin concentration drops below 7 mg/dL, which correlates with a hematocrit concentration of approximately 21%. This patient's hematocrit of 34% indicates that her hemoglobin is above the transfusion threshold.</p><p>Colonoscopy (Choice D) is indicated for patients with evidence of IDA and no clear source of bleeding. It is used primarily to rule out colorectal cancer but has the added benefit of being able to identify other causes of gastrointestinal bleeding, including arteriovenous malformations, diverticula, and hemorrhoids</p><p>Bone marrow biopsy (Choice E) is used to evaluate for malignant conditions such as multiple myeloma or leukemia, or infectious disorders in which organisms infiltrate the marrow and displace progenitor cells, such as mycobacterial infections. Bone marrow biopsy is not needed to establish the diagnosis of ACD and is unnecessarily invasive.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Delayed hemolytic transfusion reactions typically occur within 1 to 2 weeks following erythrocyte transfusion and are usually the result of pre-formed antibodies against minor erythrocyte antigens. Diagnosis is suggested by the presence of a positive Coombs test, indirect hyperbilirubinemia, and anemia.</p><p>Laboratory studies are most likely to disclose a positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test and an increased serum concentration of indirect (unconjugated) bilirubin, which is consistent with a hemolytic transfusion reaction. This patient's presentation 10 days following blood transfusion, and not immediately during transfusion, suggests a delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction (DHTR). In contrast to acute hemolytic transfusion reactions, which arise because of ABO incompatibility and can be life-threatening, DHTRs usually result from the presence of autoantibodies against minor erythrocyte antigens (eg, Rh antigens). In all cases, DHTR requires prior exposure to the antigen for the patient to produce antibodies, but in some instances, the antibodies may not be found in titers during routine blood bank screening to be appropriately recognized. The Coombs test involves incubating the patient's erythrocytes with anti-IgG and anti-complement antibody known as Coombs reagent. If an autoantibody or complement is bound to the surface of the erythrocytes, the Coombs reagent will bind to it and cause agglutination resulting in a positive test. The fixation of complement results in intravascular hemolysis and an increased indirect bilirubin while binding of antibodies to the surface of erythrocytes may mark them for removal in the reticuloendothelial system. In either case, patients present with anemia and evidence of hemolysis. Treatment is supportive.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F.</p><p>Negative Coombs test and increased direct bilirubin (Choice A) is most suggestive of a cholestasis, which may occur in conditions such as choledocholithiasis or biliary obstruction secondary to pancreatic cancer, among many other causes. This is not an expected pattern for autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AHA).</p><p>Negative Coombs test and increased indirect bilirubin (Choice B) can be seen in patients with hemolysis that is not related to antibody mediated destruction of erythrocytes. Examples include microangiopathic hemolytic anemia secondary to conditions such as disseminated intravascular coagulopathy, or hemolysis secondary to erythrocyte disorders such as sickle cell disease, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, or hereditary spherocytosis.</p><p>Negative Coombs test and normal indirect bilirubin (Choice C), or a negative Coombs test with normal bilirubin concentration (Choice F) would be expected in patients with anemia that is not related to hemolysis. Examples include iron deficiency anemia, anemia of chronic disease, anemia secondary to end-stage renal disease, or anemia secondary to infiltration of the bone marrow or destruction of erythrocyte precursors as may occur with certain malignancies and infections.</p><p>Positive Coombs test and increased direct bilirubin (Choice D) suggests two separate processes. A positive Coombs test is found in AHA while increased direct bilirubin is seen in patients with biliary stasis, which may occur as the result of intra- or extrahepatic bile duct obstruction.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypertension is the single most important preventable risk factor for TIAs and ischemic strokes. It is also an important contributor to the development of CHF, CKD, and peripheral vascular disease.</p><p>This patient's presenting symptoms of transient weakness and dysarthria or aphasia are consistent with transient ischemic attack (TIA), defined as a transient episode of neurologic dysfunction caused by focal brain, spinal cord, or retinal ischemia without evidence of infarction. Additionally, this patient presents with signs of congestive heart failure (CHF), peripheral vascular disease, and chronic kidney disease (CKD), including an enlarged point of maximal impulse, an S4 gallop, a femoral bruit, and an increased creatinine, respectively. This patient has multiple risk factors for a TIA and ischemic stroke including hypertension, diabetes mellitus, dyslipidemia, and atherosclerotic disease. Hypertension is the single most important modifiable risk factor for TIAs and ischemic strokes and is an important contributor to the development of CHF, CKD, and peripheral vascular disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Congestive heart failure (Choice A) is also associated with risk factors for ischemic stroke, such as hypertension and dyslipidemia, although it is not an independent risk factor for stroke.</p><p>Hypercholesterolemia (Choice B) has not been consistently demonstrated as a risk factor for all stroke subtypes. It may play a modest role in the development of ischemic strokes in large vessel or lacunar distributions.</p><p>Renal insufficiency (Choice D) may result from chronic hypertension but is not an independent risk factor for ischemic stroke.</p><p>Type 2 diabetes mellitus (Choice E) is an important risk factor for ischemic stroke but is not as significant as hypertension.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Alzheimer disease is the most common type of dementia, presenting with progressive cognitive deterioration that begins with short-term memory impairment and a decline in visuospatial abilities. This progresses to apraxia, language abnormalities, and dysexecutive syndrome. Age is the strongest risk factor for AD. The incidence of AD approximately doubles every 5 years after age 65.</p><p>Alzheimer disease (AD) is the most common type of dementia. It typically presents with progressive cognitive deterioration that begins with short-term memory impairment and a decline in visuospatial abilities, which progresses to apraxia, language abnormalities, and dysexecutive syndrome. The disease culminates in behavioral and personality changes that prevent the patient from performing basic activities of daily living, such as bathing. Age is the strongest risk factor for AD. The incidence of AD approximately doubles every 5 years after age 65. Less significant risk factors include hypertension, hyperlipidemia, diabetes mellitus, and a sedentary lifestyle. AD may be treated with drugs that both directly and indirectly increase acetylcholine in synaptic clefts. These include donepezil and cholinesterase inhibitors, which non-competitively and reversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase, thereby increasing the amount of synaptic acetylcholine available for neurotransmission. Though these drugs may slow the rate of cognitive decline and modestly improve functionality, they are not curative for AD.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Education level (Choice B) has been linked to AD. Some studies have demonstrated a lower incidence of AD in populations with a higher level of education. This relationship, however, is not as significant as age as a predisposing factor for AD.</p><p>Family history (Choice C) is a mild risk factor for AD. Patients with a first-degree relative with AD have a modestly increased risk for developing AD. While there are rare cases of dominantly inherited mutations that lead to early onset familial AD, most cases of AD are sporadic.</p><p>Gender (Choice D) does not directly impact the incidence of AD, although it is more prevalent in women because they have a longer life expectancy.</p><p>History of head trauma (Choice E) has not been consistently demonstrated as an independent risk factor for AD.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cluster headaches typically present with brief, unilateral, severe pain in the orbital, supraorbital, or temporal region of the head accompanied by ipsilateral autonomic symptoms such as ptosis, miosis, conjunctival injection, lacrimation, rhinorrhea, and nasal congestion.</p><p>Cluster headaches are primary headaches that typically present with brief (&lt;120 minutes), unilateral, severe pain in the orbital, supraorbital, or temporal region of the head and are accompanied by ipsilateral autonomic symptoms. The pathogenesis is incompletely understood but likely involves activation of sensory fibers of the trigeminal nerve and parasympathetic nerves. Autonomic symptoms commonly include ptosis, miosis, lacrimation, conjunctival injection, rhinorrhea, and/or nasal congestion. Patients typically present between the ages of 20 and 50 years and are often male. Cigarette and alcohol use may be triggers for patients with a history of cluster headaches. The headaches often recur over a series of weeks to months, sometimes at the same time each year. Oxygen therapy with 100% oxygen is the most effective first-line abortive treatment, and verapamil is the first-line prophylactic treatment. The clinician should always distinguish primary headache types (eg, tension, cluster, migraine) from secondary headache types (eg, intracranial bleeding, tumors, glaucoma) because the treatment and prognosis differ greatly.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (Choice B) presents with headache, nausea, vomiting, tinnitus, and transient visual obscuration or blurry vision secondary to increased intracranial pressure. External ocular appearance is normal. The presence of tearing and conjunctival injection in this patient suggests an alternate diagnosis.</p><p>Migraine (Choice C) is a common cause of headache in adult patients. It often presents with a visual aura of scintillating scotoma or fortification spectra but is not associated with signs of autonomic hyperactivity such as lacrimation or conjunctival injection.</p><p>Subdural hematoma (Choice D) may result from head trauma and the tearing of bridging veins in the subdural space, and it often presents with an acute or subacute onset headache, nausea, vomiting, and potentially with focal neurologic deficits or seizures caused by mass effect. A history of episodic symptoms over several weeks is less suggestive of an intracranial hemorrhage or hematoma.</p><p>Temporal lobe glioma (Choice E), such as a glioblastoma, are tumors of the temporal lobe and often present with headache, seizures, and vomiting. Involvement of the temporal lobe visual pathway may cause a contralateral superior quadrantanopia.</p><p>Tension-type headache (Choice F) typically presents with bilateral dull, pressure-like or band-like pain across the frontal and temporal areas. A tension-type headache does not present with signs of autonomic hyperactivity.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Seborrheic keratosis is a benign proliferation of the epidermal keratinocytes. Lesions are typically round, raised, brown or tan in color, and sharply demarcated with an adherent or stuck on appearance. The main risk factor for seborrheic keratosis is increasing age. Treatment is not usually required.</p><p>Seborrheic keratosis is a benign proliferation of the epidermis. It is characterized histologically by a benign, acanthotic proliferation of keratinocytes with absence of cellular atypia. The lesions are typically round, raised, brown or tan in color, and sharply demarcated with an adherent or stuck-on appearance. Seborrheic keratosis is common in middle-aged and older adults and is most associated with increasing age. While seborrheic keratosis is benign, the sudden appearance of numerous seborrheic keratosis lesions on the back and trunk, known as the Leser-Trélat sign, can be a paraneoplastic manifestation of gastrointestinal malignancy. No treatment is necessary for seborrheic keratosis, although lesions that are cosmetically bothersome may be removed with cryotherapy or simple excision.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Gender (Choice B) is not a significant predisposing factor for seborrheic keratosis, which affects men and women in equal proportion.</p><p>Medical history (Choice C), such as HIV in this patient, does not result in an increased risk for seborrheic keratosis. While Kaposi sarcoma is associated with AIDS, these lesions are red or purple in color and commonly occur on the face, oral mucosa, legs, and torso.</p><p>Medication history (Choice D), such as the use of antiretroviral therapy in this patient, does not result in an increased risk for seborrheic keratosis.</p><p>Occupation (Choice E) is not a significant risk factor for seborrheic keratosis. While several zoonotic diseases can affect farmworkers, such as anthrax and tularemia, these lesions are typically characterized as black eschars or ulcerations, respectively.</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Actinic keratosis is a premalignant lesion that may progress to SCC. Lack of response to topical therapy should raise suspicion for possible SCC and should prompt an excisional biopsy. Excisional biopsy is preferable because of its ability to assess for invasive disease and curative potential if the entire lesion is removed.</p><p>Actinic keratosis is a premalignant lesion that may progress to squamous cell carcinoma (SCC). Clinically, lesions typically appear as light pink, ill-defined macules with a gritty texture in areas of prolonged sun exposure, such as the face, ears, and dorsal hands. Actinic keratosis may be treated with topical medications, such as 5-fluorouracil or imiquimod, excision, cryotherapy, or dermabrasion. Ablative therapies are best used when the diagnosis is relatively certain, and a histopathologic specimen is not required. Lack of response to topical therapy should raise suspicion for possible SCC and should prompt an excisional biopsy. A low threshold of suspicion for SCC should be maintained particularly in patients with a history of inmunocompromise or solid organ transplantation. Excisional biopsy is preferable because of its ability to assess for invasive disease and curative potential if the entire lesion is removed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Observation (Choice A) is not appropriate because this patient's actinic keratosis has failed to respond to prior topical treatment, which raises suspicion for possible SCC.</p><p>Punch biopsy (Choice C) is not preferred for the diagnosis and treatment of lesions concerning for SCC because it does not allow for the complete removal of the lesion. SCC lesions that are incompletely excised usually require further excision with careful assessment of the margins.</p><p>Radiation therapy (Choice D) is not typically used for lesions that are concerning for SCC. Radiation is sometimes used in conjunction with surgical excision in high-risk SCC tumors or in lesions that are not amenable to complete excision.</p><p>Systemic chemotherapy followed by surgical excision (Choice E) is not necessary for the treatment of either actinic keratosis or SCC, both of which are usually amenable to local, topical chemotherapeutic or ablative treatments.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Septic arthritis classically presents with a painful, swollen, tender joint, generally accompanied by a fever. Diagnosis is through arthrocentesis, which may identify an organism and will generally disclose a neutrophilic-predominant exudate. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common offending organism.</p><p>Septic arthritis is most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus and presents with pain, inability to bear weight, reduced range of motion, fever, chills, nausea, myalgias, arthralgias, and fatigue. Vital signs may show fever, tachycardia, or hypotension in cases of septic shock. Physical examination typically discloses tenderness to palpation of the affected joint, a limited range of motion, pain with range of motion, joint effusion, and overlying cutaneous erythema. Potential routes of infection include direct inoculation, spread from an adjacent infection such as osteomyelitis, or hematogenous seeding. A structurally compromised joint, such as a prosthetic joint or a joint affected by years of inflammatory arthropathy may be more susceptible to infection, especially if the patient is also systemically immunocompromised. Diagnosis is through clinical examination plus arthrocentesis, which classically discloses a neutrophilic-predominant exudate with a white blood cell count of greater than 50,000/mm³. Organisms are often seen on gram stain and are detected by culture. Treatment includes intravenous antibiotics plus emergent operative debridement to prevent complications such as articular erosion, fibrous ankylosis, loss of the ability to ambulate, and the potential for sepsis and death, if severe.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Neisseria gonorrhoeae (Choice A) is a gram-negative diplococcus that is associated with cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome, disseminated gonorrhea, septic arthritis (classically disseminated with migratory polyarthritis), and urethritis.</p><p>Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Choice B) is a gram-negative rod associated with numerous clinical syndromes including urinary tract infections, osteomyelitis, otitis externa, folliculitis, hospital or ventilator-associated pneumonia, and bacteremia.</p><p>Streptococcus agalactiae (group B) (Choice D) is a gram-positive bacterium that is a common cause of neonatal infections such as meningitis, bacteremia, and pneumonia caused by vertical transmission to the fetus during childbirth. It is not a common cause of septic arthritis in adults.</p><p>Yersinia enterocolitica (Choice E) is a gram-negative bacterium that is known to cause inflammatory gastroenteritis and colitis. It classically mimics appendicitis and may present with right lower quadrant abdominal pain.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The supraspinatus tendon is most often involved in a rotator cuff injury, and generally results from overuse and repetitive motion. It presents with pain with shoulder abduction that may be improved with passive range of motion. Treatment includes rest, ice, anti-inflammatory medications, and physical therapy.</p><p>The supraspinatus tendon is the most common location of rotator cuff injury. The supraspinatus muscle lies within the supraspinous fossa along the superior portion of the scapula, and its tendon inserts on the greater tuberosity of the humerus. The supraspinatus muscle contributes to the initiation of arm abduction prior to the action of the deltoid muscle. Injury to the supraspinatus tendon may occur from degeneration, impingement, overuse, or trauma and can be evaluated through abduction of the shoulder against resistance. Classic symptoms include pain with active shoulder abduction during which time the tendon is placed under tension by the muscle, which is relieved with passive abduction as no tension is generated on the part of the patient. Treatment includes rest, ice, anti-inflammatory medications, and physical therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Biceps tendon rupture (Choice A) results in weakness of elbow flexion and forearm supination along with a retracted, prominent deformity (the retracted, contracted muscle) in the anterior aspect of the proximal arm on physical examination. The biceps muscle consists of a short head originating from the coracoid process and a long head originating from the intra-articular supraglenoid tubercle. The biceps brachii inserts at the radial tuberosity.</p><p>Cervical nerve root compression (Choice B) presents with pain in a radicular pattern associated with the compressed nerve root, which may be accompanied by paresthesia, numbness, frank muscle weakness and/or paralysis. It generally results from traumatic injury to the cervical spine, disc herniation, tumors, or degenerative joint disease and osteoarthritis of the cervical spine.</p><p>Glenohumeral subluxation (Choice C), a partial dislocation of the shoulder joint, often arises from acute or chronic disruption of the muscles, ligaments, soft tissues, and bones that comprise the joint. It presents with shoulder pain, deformity, and limited passive and active range of motion, generally following a traumatic injury sustained by the shoulder (eg, when weightlifting or playing sports). It is generally diagnosable on x-ray.</p><p>Torn glenoid labrum (Choice E) presents with shoulder pain with range of motion (especially overhead), limited range of motion, catching or locking sensation with motion, and occasional instability of the shoulder joint. It generally results from acute trauma and is diagnosed by physical examination and MRI.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Colchicine, a microtubule inhibitor, is commonly used in the management of gout, which typically presents with an acute inflammatory arthritis. Colchicine inhibits ongoing inflammation, which permits resolution of the symptoms of gout. Gout can be prevented by avoiding triggering medications and by limiting intake of purine-rich foods in addition to the use of xanthine oxidase inhibitors and medications for long-term control.</p><p>Colchicine, a microtubule inhibitor, is commonly used in the treatment of inflammatory arthropathies such as gout. It acts by preventing the activation and migration of neutrophils to a site of inflammation, though several mechanisms beyond this are proposed. Gout is an acute monoarticular arthropathy resulting from an intra-articular inflammatory reaction to the precipitation of monosodium urate crystals within the joint space. Precipitation can result from the overproduction of uric acid but more commonly occurs following the underexcretion of uric acid. Gout typically presents with acute, atraumatic single-joint pain, erythema, and swelling, which can recur in patients who are under- or untreated. The most common joint involved is the first metatarsophalangeal, as shown in the photograph, but other common joints include the knees and elbows. Diagnosis occurs through arthrocentesis, which will disclose needle-shaped, birefringent crystals. Certain medications (eg, loop diuretics), malignancy, and existing joint disease are predisposing conditions. Treatment consists of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), colchicine, and/or corticosteroids for acute exacerbations. Chronic preventive management involves the use of xanthine oxidase inhibitors such as allopurinol. Additionally, patients should avoid triggering risk factors such as purine-rich meals and heavy alcohol consumption.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Acetaminophen (Choice A) may have a role in the management of this patient's comfort and symptoms, though it would not directly treat gout or reduce inflammation in the joint itself.</p><p>Allopurinol (Choice B) inhibits xanthine oxidase, which increases the solubility of monosodium urate precursors that can then be excreted in urine. It is appropriate to initiate allopurinol for the prevention of future gout attacks, though it is generally not recommended to start during acute exacerbations.</p><p>Hydroxychloroquine (Choice D) is often used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus. Neither rheumatoid arthritis nor systemic lupus erythematosus present with birefringent, needle-like crystals on arthropathy, though both may present otherwise with joint pain and swelling.</p><p>Methotrexate (Choice E) is a versatile and often-used immunosuppressant in the management of multiple malignant, inflammatory, and autoimmune conditions such as leukemia, rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, and sarcoidosis, among others. It is also used in the medical management of ectopic pregnancy. Rheumatoid arthritis can be distinguished from gout based on presenting features and arthrocentesis.</p>", "16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Dental procedures that manipulate the gingiva or periapical teeth or violate the oral mucosa may necessitate coverage for infective endocarditis in patients with predisposing cardiac lesions. The 2020 American Heart Association guidelines recommend antibiotic prophylaxis in the setting of prosthetic cardiac valves, prosthetic implanted material such as annuloplasty rings or cords, previous infective endocarditis, unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart defects, repaired congenital heart defects with residual shunts or regurgitation adjacent to the site of a patch or prosthetic device, and in transplanted hearts with structurally abnormal valves.</p><p>No prophylaxis is indicated prior to a dental procedure in the case of a no-longer-apparent childhood murmur. Guidelines exist for the prevention of bacterial endocarditis related to dental procedures. Manipulation of oral mucosa can result in hematogenous spread of oral flora, which, in the setting of a structurally compromised heart, may promote infection and colonization of abnormal or dysfunctional endocardium. Qualifying dental procedures for which prophylaxis is recommended include any procedures in which the manipulation of gingival tissue or periapical teeth occurs, or in any case of perforation of the oral mucosa. Guidelines from the American Heart Association recommend antibiotic prophylaxis in the setting of prosthetic cardiac valves, prosthetic implanted material such as annuloplasty rings or cords, previous infective endocarditis, unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart defects, repaired congenital heart defects with residual shunts or regurgitation adjacent to the site of a patch or prosthetic device, and in transplanted hearts with structurally abnormal valves. Noncyanotic congenital heart defects such as ventricular septal defects, especially if resolved, do not require antibiotic prophylaxis unless another indication is present. When indicated, antibiotics that cover oral flora should be administered 30 to 60 minutes before the time of the procedure as a single dose.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Oral amoxicillin 1 hour before the dental procedure (Choice A) would be correct in cases where prophylaxis is indicated, provided there are no contraindications to the antibiotic (eg, allergy). Second line antibiotics in cases of allergy include clindamycin, macrolides, and cephalosporins.</p><p>Oral amoxicillin 24 hours before the procedure (Choice B) would be inappropriate because peak serum concentrations would have passed by the time of the procedure.</p><p>Intravenous vancomycin 1 hour before the dental procedure (Choice C) and intravenous vancomycin 24 hours before the dental procedure (Choice D) are both incorrect because vancomycin offers poor coverage of gram-negative and anaerobic organisms, which can also cause infective endocarditis.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Aortic atherosclerosis may present with claudication of the buttocks and lower extremities as well as lower extremity pain, paresthesia, paraplegia, and erectile dysfunction. Treatment includes medical modification of risk factors along with reperfusion by surgical or endovascular methods.</p><p>Aortic atherosclerosis commonly occurs at the aortic arch, where it serves as a major risk factor for cerebral embolism, and in the abdominal aorta distal to the renal arteries. Aortic atherosclerosis may lead to ischemia of the lower extremities, presenting with claudication of the buttocks and legs, lower extremity pain, paresthesia, paraplegia, shiny, hairless skin, and erectile dysfunction. A careful examination with attention to lower extremity pulses is crucial to distinguish an acute or chronic ischemic cause from potential neurologic causes. The diagnosis of aortic atherosclerosis is supported by CT or MR angiography along with assessment of ankle brachial blood pressure indices. Prompt reperfusion is essential to prevent further loss of function caused by hypoperfusion and can be accomplished with balloon or stent angioplasty, aortic endarterectomy, vascular bypass surgery (eg, aortofemoral bypass grafting), or thrombolysis or thrombectomy in cases of acute plaque rupture or embolic occlusion. Modifiable risk factors for aortic atherosclerotic disease include smoking, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, diabetes mellitus, and cocaine use.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Aortic aneurysm (Choice A) typically presents in male smokers with a family history and a personal history of atherosclerotic disease. Other risk factors include hypercholesterolemia, hypertension, and trauma. It may present as a palpable, pulsatile abdominal mass if large and in a thin patient. Claudication is not a typical symptom. Aortic aneurysms are generally asymptomatic until they rupture, which generally presents with acute abdominal pain and hypotension and is often fatal if not promptly identified and surgically managed.</p><p>Aortic dissection (Choice B) typically presents as tearing chest or abdominal pain radiating to the back, often with a peripheral pulse or blood pressure deficit, and with neurologic symptoms such as numbness or tingling in an extremity. It can present with mesenteric ischemia if a splanchnic vessel is involved or with renal infarction if a renal artery is involved.</p><p>Coarctation of the aorta (Choice D) refers to a narrowing of the aorta. It is commonly associated with a bicuspid aortic valve and Turner syndrome. It typically presents during childhood with a systolic murmur along with differential pulses and blood pressures between extremities. If untreated, left ventricular hypertrophy with resultant heart failure may result.</p><p>Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease) (Choice E) is a small-vessel vasculitis associated with smoking. It typically presents with claudication symptoms that may progress to painful ulceration, gangrene, and autoamputation of digits. Ulcerations tend to occur in the tips of the fingers and toes.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The presence of a large-volume or rapidly accumulating fluid in the pericardial space can cause cardiac tamponade, an obstructive shock state resulting from impaired venous return and poor right ventricular filling. Malignancy is a common predisposing condition and treatment includes emergent pericardial decompression to restore chamber filling.</p><p>Fluid that accumulates within the pericardial space in a rapid-onset or large-volume fashion can result in pericardial tamponade. Compression of the cardiac chambers, especially the right ventricle, impairs cardiac filling with resultant decreased cardiac output caused by low stroke volume. Severe cases result in obstructive shock, manifest by tachycardia and hypotension. Physical examination findings include Beck triad (hypotension, distended jugular veins, and distant heart sounds). ECG may disclose low voltage and/or electrical alternans, and there may be pulsus paradoxus on evaluation of blood pressure. Treatment requires emergent pericardiocentesis to restore diastolic filling and cardiac output. Predisposing conditions include but are not limited to malignancy, infectious or inflammatory states such as tuberculosis, viral pericarditis, and systemic lupus erythematosus, traumatic injury, and aortic dissection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, and F.</p><p>Aorta (Choice A) can be implicated in cases of pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade if retrograde travel of an aortic dissection results in rupture into the pericardial space. Such cases generally present with acute chest pain radiating to the back, asymmetric pulses or extremity blood pressures, and potential neurologic deficits.</p><p>Endocardium (Choice B) is involved in cases of endocarditis, typically from an infectious and inflammatory state. Right-sided endocarditis often involves the tricuspid valve following seeding during intravenous drug abuse. It classically presents with fever, septic emboli, vascular and immunologic phenomena such as splinter hemorrhages, Osler nodes, and Janeway lesions. Inflammatory endocarditis can occur in systemic lupus erythematosus, such as in Libman-Sacks endocarditis, but is less common than pericarditis as a cardiac manifestation of the condition.</p><p>Myocardium (Choice C) is implicated in cases of myocarditis, which may be infectious or inflammatory, and generally presents with chest discomfort, shortness of breath, tachycardia, and signs of heart failure on examination.</p><p>Pulmonary artery (Choice E) is involved in cases of pulmonary embolism, which presents with chest discomfort, shortness of breath, signs of right heart failure or strain such as lower extremity edema, hepatomegaly, and jugular venous distention. Signs of deep venous thrombosis are also often present, such as asymmetric pitting edema of a tender extremity.</p><p>Pulmonary veins (Choice F) may be affected by left-sided heart failure, congestion of which leads to cardiogenic pulmonary edema. This presents with shortness of breath, cough often associated with frothy sputum, and crackles on lung examination.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pericardial effusions generally arise from infectious, inflammatory, traumatic, malignant, or vascular predisposing conditions. Treatment depends on the severity of presentation and can range from watchful waiting to emergent pericardial decompression in cases of tamponade.</p><p>The presentation of pericardial effusion may range from asymptomatic to severe, symptomatic cardiac tamponade in large-volume or rapidly accumulating cases. Compression of the cardiac chambers, especially the right ventricle, impairs cardiac filling and decreases cardiac output (low stroke volume). Presenting features include but are not limited to a history of chest discomfort, shortness of breath, decreased exercise tolerance, fluid retention, and lightheadedness, along with any historical feature that may explain the cause of effusion (eg, weight loss or history of malignancy in cases of malignant effusion). Vital signs may be normal in cases of small effusions or may include tachycardia and hypotension in cases of tamponade. Physical examination findings include Beck triad (hypotension, distended jugular veins, and distant heart sounds) in the case of tamponade. These findings occur because of the presence of fluid in the pericardial space muffling heart sounds and increasing collapsing pressure on the right ventricle, which is transmitted to the vena cavae and their tributaries. In such a manner, increased right heart pressure is reflected as increased jugular venous distention (JVD). ECG may disclose low voltage and/or electrical alternans. There may be pulsus paradoxus on evaluation of blood pressure. Confirmatory diagnosis requires visualization of the effusion, often on echocardiogram. Mild or asymptomatic cases may be managed conservatively with watchful waiting and treatment of any predisposing conditions, whereas moderate, symptomatic, or severe cases, especially if accompanied by hemodynamic instability require treatment with pericardiocentesis or a pericardial window to restore diastolic filling and cardiac output. Predisposing conditions include but are not limited to malignancy, infectious or inflammatory states such as tuberculosis, pericarditis, or systemic lupus erythematosus, traumatic injury, and aortic dissection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Anterior myocardial infarct (Choice A) generally presents with acute chest pain, shortness of breath, fatigue, neck, jaw, or arm pain. ECG findings include ST elevations in the precordial leads, generally V1-V4 with reciprocal change in opposing leads. Electrical alternans and low voltage would not be expected unless an effusion were present. Right ventricular myocardial infarction (Choice E) generally presents with inferior (II, III, aVF) and right-sided (V4R) ST elevations on ECG.</p><p>Cor pulmonale (Choice B) refers to right heart failure secondary to chronic pulmonary pathology with increased pulmonary vascular resistance and right-sided heart pressures. It can result from many conditions such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, interstitial lung disease, thromboembolic disease, systemic scleroderma, or primary pulmonary hypertension. It may present with JVD and edema but would not generally present with electrical alternans and low voltage on ECG. ECG findings may include signs of right ventricular hypertrophy.</p><p>Pulmonary emboli (Choice D) classically present acutely with chest pain (often pleuritic) and shortness of breath. Predisposing features include hypercoagulability and immobilization. The physical examination is often normal, though occasionally signs of right heart strain can be detected, such as JVD and peripheral edema. The ECG may disclose normal sinus rhythm or sinus tachycardia, right axis deviation, signs of right heart strain such as right bundle branch blocks and right ventricular hypertrophy, or the S1Q3T3 pattern.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Aortic dissection classically presents with severe, acute, tearing chest or abdominal pain radiating to the back and differential blood pressures between extremities with asymmetric pulses. Complications and additional presenting features depend on the involvement of branch vessels (eg, stroke resulting from carotid occlusion, mesenteric ischemia from mesenteric arterial occlusion, renal infarction from renal artery occlusion). Treatment involves fluid resuscitation along with rapidly lowering blood pressure and heart rate to prevent advancement of the dissection flap and surgical or procedural intervention, if appropriate.</p><p>Aortic dissection classically presents with severe, acute, tearing chest or abdominal pain radiating to the back and differential blood pressures between extremities with asymmetric pulses. Dissection begins with a disruption of the tunica intima of the vessel, which permits blood to dissect the layers of the vessel wall with each heartbeat. A dissection flap may advance in any direction within the vessel, occluding or involving branch ostia. If vessel takeoffs supplying the limbs are affected, pulse deficits or differential blood pressures may arise. If splanchnic vessels are involved, end-organ dysfunction such as mesenteric ischemia or acute kidney injury may be seen. Management may be medical or surgical and depends on the location of the dissection within the vessel (eg, Type A vs Type B). Regardless of the need for surgery, all patients with dissection should receive immediate medical management to prevent further progression of the flap. Controlling the patient's heart rate and blood pressure is the priority in preventing progression. Intravenous β-adrenergic blocking medications should be used with a goal of lowering the heart rate to approximately 60 beats per minute and the blood pressure to less than 100 to 120 mm Hg systolic and 60 to 80 mm Hg diastolic. Lowering the heart rate helps prevent the advancement of the dissection flap and resultant occlusion of branch vessels by limiting the pulse pressure differential. Complications such as stroke, acute coronary syndrome, and cardiac tamponade may be immediately life-threatening.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>lliac occlusive disease (Leriche syndrome) (Choice A) may present in acute or chronic fashion. Acute iliac occlusion generally presents with a cool, painful, pale lower extremity, motor weakness, paresthesia, pulse deficit, and differential blood pressure. Chronic occlusive disease may present with claudication, hair loss, shiny skin, atrophy, and impotence.</p><p>Renal artery embolus (Choice B) may present with abdominal, flank, or back pain and costovertebral angle tenderness on examination, decreased urine output, or hematuria in cases of resultant infarction. It would not generally present with symptoms involving the extremities.</p><p>Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm (Choice C) classically presents with tearing abdominal pain radiating to the back and a pulsatile abdominal mass in an older person with history of smoking, hypertension, connective tissue disease, or known atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Rupture first occurs into the retroperitoneal space, where it may remain transiently contained. If exsanguination continues, rupture may involve the peritoneal cavity, at which time rapid and unmitigated exsanguination can occur. Similarly, a ruptured thoracic aneurysm (Choice D) presents with tearing chest pain radiating to the back, though unless branch vessels are involved, the physical examination may be normal apart from the variable presence of a murmur. The most common presentation of both aneurysms, which are often occult for years, is at the time of rupture, at which time they may present with severe pain and signs of hemorrhagic shock. Treatment includes fluid resuscitation and/or blood transfusion, along with emergent operative or procedural repair.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Anticoagulation is indicated in cases of atrial fibrillation to prevent the formation of atrial thrombus, which reduces the risk for thromboembolic complications such as strokes, mesenteric ischemia, and limb ischemia. Warfarin and direct oral anticoagulants (eg, apixaban) are commonly employed agents.</p><p>Atrial fibrillation describes the uncoordinated contraction of the cardiac atria and is often the result of chronic left atrial enlargement caused by mitral regurgitation or increased left sided pressures (eg, in left ventricular hypertrophy with diastolic dysfunction). When the atria do not uniformly fill and contract, blood can become stagnant in areas of the atria, especially in the left atrial appendage. Blood in stasis is prone to coagulate, which results in the formation of a thrombus within the cardiac chambers. If a portion of such a thrombus embolizes, many clinical syndromes can result. For example, strokes occur when a thromboembolism occludes cerebral circulation, mesenteric ischemia occurs when the mesenteric arteries are occluded, and acute limb ischemia occurs when an artery supplying an extremity becomes occluded. Management of atrial fibrillation includes anticoagulation with warfarin or a direct oral anticoagulant (eg, apixaban) to prevent the formation of atrial thrombus, which reduces the risk for thromboembolic complications.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Aspirin therapy (Choice A) is indicated in the prevention of acute coronary syndromes and cerebrovascular accidents. It has not been shown to reduce the risk for thromboembolic complications in atrial fibrillation. Commonly, patients with atrial fibrillation are at increased risk for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease, many of whom will therefore be on aspirin (an anti-platelet agent) in addition to an anticoagulant.</p><p>Digoxin therapy (Choice B) is indicated in the management of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and is also sometimes used for rate control in atrial fibrillation in cases where β-adrenergic blockers or calcium channel blockers are insufficient. It does not directly influence the coagulation cascade.</p><p>Verapamil therapy (Choice C) is indicated in the management of heart rate in atrial fibrillation, though it does not act on the coagulation cascade and would not prevent the formation or embolization of thrombi.</p><p>Mitral valve replacement (Choice E) is appropriate in cases of severe or symptomatic mitral stenosis or regurgitation, however in the setting of left atrial enlargement and existing atrial fibrillation, replacing the valve generally does not reverse the presence of atrial fibrillation. Additionally, anticoagulation is indicated even in cases of paroxysmal atrial fibrillation because spontaneous conversion from sinus rhythm to fibrillation may be undetected by patients.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hospital-acquired pneumonia arises because of pathogenic introduction into the tracheobronchial tree, such as in the setting of intubation or aspiration. Pathogens are often aggressive and resistant, and include MRSA and P. aeruginosa, among others. Treatment with early broad-spectrum antibiotics is indicated.</p><p>Pneumonia classically presents with acute onset cough (generally productive of sputum), shortness of breath, chest discomfort, fever, chills, and fatigue. Vital signs may show fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypoxemia, and hypotension (if severe). On examination, rhonchi and rales may be detected in consolidated lung, along with egophony and dullness to percussion. Patients may have increased work of breathing or respiratory distress. The diagnosis is supported radiographically with airspace disease or consolidation visualized on x-ray, as shown here, or on CT. Pneumonia arises because of the presence of bacteria, virus, parasite, or fungi within pulmonary parenchyma, triggering an inflammatory reaction with resultant exudate into airspace. It is common in hospitalized patients because of risk factors for introduction of a pathogen, aspiration, and immunocompromise, including recent surgery, swallowing dysfunction, and intubation. Additionally, resistant pathogens often colonize hospitals and equipment, increasing the risk for refractory infection despite coadministration of antibiotics. Treatment of hospital-acquired pneumonia generally begins with the administration of broad-spectrum antibiotics (especially those covering methicillin resistant S. aureus [MRSA] and P. aeruginosa) while awaiting culture and sensitivity data from sputum samples if obtained. Suitable antibiotics for this purpose include vancomycin and third- or fourth-generation cephalosporins such as ceftazidime or cefepime. Antibiotics should then be narrowed to those that suitably target the infecting agent once a clinical response is achieved. Reduction in risk factors should occur, such as upright positioning of patients, removal of any unnecessary pharyngeal lines or tubes (eg, nasogastric tubes), and frequent respiratory therapy/pulmonary toileting. Postoperative pain, often worse when breathing, should be aggressively managed to permit full chest excursion, promote coughing and respiratory clearance, and limit atelectasis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Administration of ceftriaxone and azithromycin (Choice A) is appropriate in the setting of community-acquired pneumonia (CAP). Common pathogens in CAP include S. pneumoniae, and atypical organisms such as L. pneumophila and M. pneumoniae. Neither of these agents sufficiently cover common hospital-acquired pathogens.</p><p>Administration of sulfamethoxazole and cefuroxime (Choice B) may not be sufficiently broad to cover aggressive or resistant pathogens found in hospital-acquired infections.</p><p>Bronchoscopy for microbiologic assessment (Choice D) may be necessary when initial treatment of infection does not lead to clinical improvement, or when there is diagnostic uncertainty as to the suspected pathogen at hand. Empiric treatment in uncomplicated cases generally precedes bronchoscopy. In general, induced, expectorated, or aspirated samples can be obtained. Such methods are less invasive and resource-consuming than bronchoscopy, though such samples may be contaminated with oropharyngeal flora making microbiologic diagnosis more challenging.</p><p>CT scan of the chest with pulmonary embolism protocol (Choice E) is appropriate in cases of suspected pulmonary embolism, which presents with pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypoxia, and with signs of deep vein thrombosis on examination. Unless lung parenchyma is infarcted, lung sounds are otherwise normal.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Advanced lung cancer can obstruct a bronchus, causing atelectasis, hemoptysis, and post-obstructive recurrent pneumonia. When large, invasive, or metastatic, the prognosis of lung cancer is worse. As a result, screening is indicated to promote early detection of disease.</p><p>Lung cancer often remains asymptomatic until advanced stage, at which time the tumor has locally invaded or metastasized, leading to the development of symptoms. The initial presentation is often nonspecific, including weight loss and fatigue. Once the tumor has invaded or involved neurosensory and vascular structures, patients may begin to experience coughing and hemoptysis. If the lesion is large enough to invade or obstruct a bronchus, patients may also experience post-obstructive or recurrent pneumonia in parenchyma distal to the lesion, caused by atelectasis and impaired endobronchial clearance of mucus, secretions, and inhaled pathogens. In this patient, the decreased breath sounds over the right posterior hemithorax suggest the presence of an endobronchial obstruction to airflow; this is further supported by atelectasis on x-ray in the corresponding area. Squamous cell carcinoma and small cell carcinoma of the lung tend to be centrally occurring lesions, often seen in smokers. Non-small cell carcinoma and other subtypes are more often peripherally occurring and may be more common in nonsmokers. Because of the significant morbidity and mortality with late diagnosis, screening low-dose chest CT is recommended by the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force in patients aged 50 to 80 years with a 20 pack-year smoking history and who currently smoke or have quit within the past 15 years.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Increased pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (Choice B) describes the pathophysiology of cardiogenic pulmonary edema: fluid accumulating in the interstitial and alveolar spaces of the lung because of increased left-heart pressures. On examination, rales may be heard, generally in dependent areas. X-ray generally discloses bilateral airspace edema, cephalization of the vessels, Kerley B lines, pleural effusions, and cardiomegaly.</p><p>Pleural inflammation with fluid formation (Choice C) describes the pathophysiology of pleurisy, mesothelioma, empyema, or similar extra-pulmonary pathology. While lung sounds would be reduced in such cases, a pleural effusion would be visible on imaging, as opposed to focal atelectasis of lung.</p><p>Proximal airway inflammation (Choice D) describes the pathophysiology of bronchitis, an acute, frequently viral infection of the bronchi. Patients present with acute productive cough, chest discomfort, and shortness of breath, and occasional fever. Bronchitis often follows a viral upper respiratory infection. Weight loss would be atypical, though patients may occasionally experience hemoptysis caused by inflammation of the airway. X-rays in bronchitis are generally unremarkable unless mucus plugging is present, in which case atelectasis may be present.</p><p>Pulmonary parenchymal necrosis (Choice E) may arise as a consequence of infarction (eg, in pulmonary embolism), vasculitis, or infection. It would generally present in acute fashion or with symptoms associated with its cause. Weight loss and a subacute to chronic time-course would be atypical.</p><p>Pulmonary vascular obstruction (Choice F) describes pulmonary embolism, which presents with acute chest pain, shortness of breath, or hemoptysis, in the setting of known or suspected deep venous thrombosis (thromboembolism), catheterization (air embolism), long bone fracture (fat embolism), or pregnancy (amniotic fluid embolism). Collapsed lung and weight loss would be unlikely features.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Asthma presents with a reversible, obstructive pattern of PFTs, which includes decreases in FEV1, decreased FEV1:FVC ratio, and preservation of FVC. Flow volume loops generally disclose abrupt decreases in the expiratory flow rate shortly after the initiation of exhalation. With treatment and resolution of the obstruction, PFTs and flow-volume loops should approximate those of an unaffected individual.</p><p>Asthma, a reversible obstructive lung disease, presents with shortness of breath, cough, tachypnea, and hypoxemia (in severe cases), increased work of breathing, and wheezing on examination. It is often triggered by exercise, allergens, illness, and smoke. It is characterized by a reversible bronchospasm which causes obstruction of airflow. Generally, exhalation is affected more than inhalation. The diagnosis is suspected based on clinical history and examination, and it is confirmed through pulmonary function tests (PFTs) which disclose an obstructive pattern characterized by a decrease in FEV1, decreased FEV1:FVC ratio, and preservation of FVC. Flow volume loops generally disclose abrupt decreases in the expiratory flow rate compared to those of an unaffected patient, which creates a concave appearance of the expiratory flow loop as initial expiratory flow rates rapidly decrease in the setting of obstruction. Asthma can also be diagnosed based on reversibility of bronchospasm — using β-adrenergic agonists or methacholine, PFTs will disclose alleviated (β-adrenergic agonist) or exacerbated (methacholine) obstruction. Treatment depends on the severity of asthma, with cases of intermittent asthma requiring bronchodilators as needed and elimination of triggers. Persistent asthma is treated with corticosteroids and long-acting bronchodilators, the route and choice of which depends on the severity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Ankylosing spondylitis (Choice A) presents with progressive skeletal rigidity of the spine which limits chest excursion during breathing, resulting in a pattern of restrictive lung disease on PFTs, marked by decreases in total lung capacity (TLC), FVC, and functional residual capacity (FRC), and residual volume (RV), with preservation of the FEV1:FVC ratio.</p><p>Cystic fibrosis (Choice C) generally presents in childhood and affects multiple body systems. Pulmonary effects arise because of thick bronchial mucus preventing clearance of inhaled pathogens and leading to chronic bronchial inflammation, dilation, air trapping, and scarring, known as bronchiectasis. PFTs and flow volume loops generally reflect an obstructive pattern.</p><p>Emphysema (Choice D) presents in a manner like asthma, with obstructive lung disease; however, the response to bronchodilators may be limited compared to asthma. Emphysema results from the loss of lung elasticity, which limits the degree to which small airways will be tethered open, leading to a generally irreversible obstructive pattern on PFTs and flow volume loops.</p><p>Extrathoracic airway obstruction (Choice E), when fixed, limits airflow in both inspiratory and expiratory phases. Such obstruction can arise from conditions such as malignancy, hematoma, or stricture. PFTs reflect decreased FEV1, however over a sufficiently long expiratory window would show comparable FVC and TLC. Rate of airflow is limited because of increased resistance from airway obstruction. Flow volume loops display lower peak flow rates and flattening in expiratory and inspiratory loops.</p><p>Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) (Choice F) is characterized by increased connective and stromal tissue in the lung. The presence of this tissue restricts the available airspace within the lung but promotes increased tissue elasticity, limiting obstruction. IPF results in a restrictive pattern on PFTs, characterized by decreases in TLC, FVC, and FRC, and RV, with preservation of or even increases in the FEV1:FVC ratio.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pneumoconiosis describes interstitial lung disease arising because of inhalation of small solid particulate matter such as dust or powder that leads to the development of fibrosis with resultant restrictive lung disease. Diagnosis is based on clinical features, exposure, imaging showing fibrosis, and pulmonary function tests showing a restrictive pattern of lung disease. A key feature of restrictive lung disease on pulmonary function testing is the preserved or increased ratio of FEV1:FVC.</p><p>Pneumoconiosis describes interstitial lung disease arising because of inhalation of small solid particulate matter such as dust or powder (eg, talc, silica, beryllium, and ores) that leads to the development of fibrosis with resultant restrictive lung disease. It is often seen in patients with chronic exposures, such as foundry workers, miners, shipyard workers, cotton workers, farmers, stone drillers, and sandblasters. The disease's progress is often worsened by smoking. It generally presents with chronic symptoms of cough and shortness of breath on exertion; when severe or advanced, it can be associated with the need for supplemental oxygen and symptoms of heart failure (eg, cor pulmonale). Diagnosis is based on clinical history, symptoms, examination, exposure history, characteristic imaging tests that show fibrosis (x-ray may show honeycombing and fibrosis, especially in the apices), and pulmonary function tests that disclose a restrictive pattern of lung disease marked by decreased overall volumes and capacities but a preserved or increased ratio of FEV1:FVC. This ratio is a key distinguishing feature from obstructive lung disease, which generally presents with a decrease in the ratio.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Asbestosis (Choice A) arises because of chronic exposure to inhaled asbestos, which may cause restrictive lung disease and pneumoconiosis, with similar pulmonary function tests showing a restrictive pattern. However, on imaging, asbestosis often causes fibrosis of dependent and inferior portions of lung and is also associated with pleural thickening and nodularity.</p><p>Aspergillosis (Choice B) can result in many different clinical presentations such as aspergillomas, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, chronic necrotizing aspergillosis, and airway invasive or angioinvasive aspergillosis. The most common are aspergillomas, which are fungal masses that arise within existing cavitary lesions of the lung, such as those caused by previous infection with tuberculosis, emphysematous blebs or bullae, or from cavitary squamous cell lung carcinoma.</p><p>Asthma (Choice C), bronchiectasis (Choice D), and chronic bronchitis (Choice E) all present with an obstructive pattern on pulmonary function testing. An obstructive pattern is characterized by a decrease in FEV1, decreased FEV1:FVC ratio, and preservation of FVC. In general, these conditions also present with wheezing and air trapping on examination, as opposed to crackles.</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pleural effusions occur because of multiple underlying conditions including infection such as empyema or pneumonia, malignancy such as lung cancer or mesothelioma, and increased hydrostatic pressure such as left-sided heart failure. Pleural fluid analysis can support or disclose a diagnosis. Empyema is characterized by an exudative effusion with increased pleural fluid leukocytes, decreased pH, decreased glucose, increased protein, and increased lactate dehydrogenase.</p><p>Pleural effusions occur because of multiple underlying conditions including infection such as empyema, pneumonia, malignancy such as lung cancer or mesothelioma, and increased hydrostatic pressure such as left-sided heart failure. They are clinically suspected when examination discloses dullness to percussion and diminished breath sounds, and they can be visualized on imaging including chest x-ray, computerized tomography, or ultrasonography. Clinical features such as immunocompromise, history of malignancy, or infectious symptoms or signs of sepsis such as fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, and lethargy can suggest the cause. Analyzing the pleural fluid can further inform the diagnosis. Pleural fluid is analyzed for cell count, organisms, pH, glucose, total protein, and enzyme concentrations. Decreased pH and decreased glucose concentration are indicative of increased cellular metabolism in the fluid and may indicate underlying malignancy and/or infection. Fluid can be further categorized as transudate (serous fluid resulting from imbalanced hydrostatic or oncotic pressure) or exudate (complex fluid resulting from pneumonia, empyema, malignancy, or inflammatory disease) using Light's criteria, which compares the pleural fluid protein and lactate dehydrogenase concentrations to those found in serum. Empyema is characterized by an exudative effusion with increased pleural fluid leukocytes, decreased pH, decreased glucose, increased protein, and increased lactate dehydrogenase. It is a known complication of pneumonia, which presents with cough, fever, tachypnea, and infiltrate on chest x-ray. Management of empyema includes evacuation via chest tube or surgery in addition to antibiotic therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Malignancy (Choice B) may present with exudative pleural effusion and an infiltrate on chest x-ray, though unless superinfection is present, would not generally present with acute fever and cough. Additionally, pleural fluid may contain malignant cells, and would be less likely to contain predominantly neutrophils as primary cell type.</p><p>Rheumatoid arthritis (Choice C) may cause pleural effusions in the setting of associated serositis. Unless superinfection is present, fever would be uncommon. Pleural fluid in rheumatoid arthritis is generally exudative, and its characteristics including neutrophil predominance can appear like empyema.</p><p>Tuberculosis (Choice D) frequently causes pleural effusions, which are among the more common extrapulmonary forms of the disease. Effusions are generally exudative, though organisms may be detected on acid-fast stain. When suspected, pleural fluid adenosine deaminase concentration should be assessed, which is often increased. Tubercular effusions are often lymphocyte predominant.</p><p>Viral infection (Choice E) is an uncommon cause of pleural effusion and would not generally present with a focal lobar or segmental area of airspace disease on x-ray. Viral pneumonia often presents with normal x-ray or with areas of diffuse, patchy airspace disease in multiple lung fields.</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Crohn disease may manifest with steatorrhea caused by the impairment of bile salt resorption secondary to inflammation of the terminal ileum. Bile salts play a crucial role in the solubilization of fats in the enteric lumen, and are recycled through the enterohepatic circulation, which is dependent on the resorption of bile salts through an intact ileal mucosa. Impairment of bile salt resorption caused by ileal inflammation can overwhelm the ability of the liver to synthesize de novo bile acids, resulting in deficiency in fat-soluble vitamin absorption.</p><p>Crohn disease is characterized by transmural inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract, most frequently involving the terminal ileum. It can arise anywhere in the gastrointestinal tract and is a subclass of inflammatory bowel disease. Most patients experience chronic abdominal cramping, bloody diarrhea, and weight loss. Examination often shows generalized abdominal tenderness, sometimes worse in the right lower quadrant, and extraintestinal manifestations such as uveitis. Severe Crohn disease can result in intestinal obstruction from stricture or inflammation, bowel perforation, intra-abdominal abscess, and the formation of fistulas. Crohn disease may manifest with steatorrhea caused by the impairment of bile salt resorption secondary to inflammation of the terminal ileum. Bile salts play a crucial role in the solubilization of fats in the enteric lumen, and are recycled through the enterohepatic circulation, which is dependent on the resorption of bile salts through an intact ileal mucosa. Impairment of bile salt resorption because of ileal inflammation can overwhelm the ability of the liver to synthesize de novo bile acids, resulting in a deficiency in fat-soluble vitamin absorption. Patients with Crohn disease should undergo frequent evaluation for deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Bacterial overgrowth (Choice A) of the small bowel may occur in the setting of prior abdominal surgeries or prolonged proton-pump inhibitor use. This patient's presentation is more suggestive of fat malabsorption related to Crohn disease.</p><p>Coexisting pancreatic exocrine dysfunction (Choice B) may rarely occur in association with inflammatory bowel disease, caused either by direct pancreatic inflammation or by inflammation of the intestinal wall near the pancreatic duct. However, this complication occurs less frequently than steatorrhea secondary to ileal inflammation and associated bile salt deficiency.</p><p>Defective release of secretin (Choice C) does not commonly occur in the setting of Crohn disease. Secretin is produced by duodenal S cells and promotes the release of bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretions and bile, while inhibiting gastric acid production.</p><p>Poor mixing of bile salts with dietary lipids (Choice E) is incorrect. Deficient resorption of bile salts, rather than defective formation of micelles, is the primary cause of steatorrhea in patients with Crohn disease.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Sialadenitis refers to inflammation of the salivary gland and typically presents with acute onset of pain and swelling over the affected gland. Acute sialadenitis most commonly occurs because of obstruction of the gland caused by sialolithiasis but may also occur secondary to viral infection (eg, mumps of Epstein-Barr virus), bacterial infection (most commonly with S. aureus), or strictures following radiotherapy. Treatment is primarily conservative with analgesics, application of heat and massage, and hydration.</p><p>Sialadenitis refers to inflammation of the salivary gland and typically presents with acute onset of pain and swelling over the affected gland. Acute sialadenitis often persists for several weeks and most commonly occurs because of obstruction of the gland caused by sialolithiasis but may also occur secondary to viral infection (eg, mumps or Epstein-Barr virus), bacterial infection (most commonly with S. aureus), or strictures following radiotherapy. The presence of constitutional symptoms may be indicative of a viral cause, while the expression of purulent discharge from the glandular orifice may indicate a bacterial infection. This patient's history of pain initially precipitated by meals is most suggestive of the presence of a salivary gland stone causing intermittent obstruction. Further evaluation with ultrasonography or CT may help establish the diagnosis. Treatment is primarily conservative with analgesics, application of heat and massage, and hydration. Patients that fail to improve with conservative management sometimes require removal of a sialolith, if present, usually by sialoendoscopy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Mumps (Choice A) presents with salivary gland swelling, which is typically bilateral and accompanied by other signs and symptoms, such as fever, headache, malaise, and myalgia. Mumps typically presents in children and is rare in the United States because of widespread vaccination.</p><p>Sarcoidosis (Choice B) may present with parotid gland inflammation. This presentation forms part of the classic Heerfordt syndrome, along with anterior uveitis, facial nerve palsy, and fever. This manifestation of sarcoidosis is uncommon, and this patient lacks the other typical signs of Heerfordt syndrome.</p><p>Sjogren syndrome (Choice D) is an autoimmune disorder characterized by chronic inflammation and destruction of the lacrimal and salivary glands. Salivary involvement may present with chronic, painless, bilateral salivary gland enlargement.</p><p>Temporomandibular joint dysfunction (Choice E) may produce symptoms of periauricular pain and popping sensations with chewing. It does not cause facial swelling.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Biliary colic is caused by transitory obstruction of the cystic duct by gallstones and presents with dull, colicky right upper quadrant pain that is triggered by meals and potentially radiates to the right shoulder. Most episodes resolve spontaneously. Elective cholecystectomy is recommended to prevent complications from the gallstones.</p><p>This patient's presenting symptom of colicky pain in the right upper quadrant is consistent with biliary colic secondary to transitory obstruction of the cystic duct by gallstones. The pain is typically dull, severe, triggered by meals, and may radiate to the right shoulder. If the gallstone is passed into the common bile duct, biliary obstruction may ensue and lead to jaundice, hepatic injury, and/or acute cholangitis. Most episodes of biliary colic last for several hours before subsiding spontaneously. Biliary colic can be distinguished from cholecystitis by a lack of fever, a normal abdominal examination, and a lack of leukocytosis. Abdominal ultrasonography can aid the diagnosis by identifying the presence of gallstones. Provided that the clinical picture and examination are improving, the acute management of biliary colic is generally conservative with oral analgesics. Elective cholecystectomy is recommended to prevent future complications from the gallstones. If symptoms of biliary colic progress to show features of jaundice, hepatic injury, cholecystitis, pancreatitis, choledocholithiasis, or cholangitis, operative (cholecystectomy) or interventional (eg, endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography) management should take place.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Bacterial growth in bile within the common hepatic duct (Choice A) may result from an ascending infection of the biliary tree, known as cholangitis, which typically occurs in the setting of biliary instrumentation or choledocholithiasis. Acute cholangitis presents with fever and jaundice in addition to abdominal pain (Charcot triad) and possibly with shock and altered mental status (Reynolds pentad).</p><p>Bacterial invasion of the gallbladder wall (Choice B) results in acute cholecystitis, which presents with fever, abdominal pain, and tenderness to palpation in the right upper quadrant, often in a patient with a history of gallstones. It is occasionally preceded by symptoms of biliary colic. Patients typically also present with leukocytosis.</p><p>Transitory chemical irritation of the gallbladder wall (Choice C) may occur in the setting of intra-arterial chemotherapeutic embolization of hepatic metastases, typically performed by interventional radiology. Collateral and/or aberrant anatomic arterial flow may result in the inadvertent dosage of chemotherapeutic agents to the gallbladder, which results in transient inflammatory symptoms that typically self-resolve. The presence of gallstones and the colicky nature of this patient's pain are more consistent with biliary colic.</p><p>Transitory obstruction of the common hepatic duct by gallstones (Choice D) may occur in the setting of choledocholithiasis and would also present with increased bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Recurrent episodes of acute pancreatitis may lead to chronic pancreatitis and insufficiency of both the exocrine and endocrine pancreas. The loss of endogenous insulin leads to the development of diabetes mellitus, with symptoms including polyuria, polydipsia, and polyphagia. In this scenario, the pancreatic production of insulin and glucagon are decreased, while peripheral insulin response is normal.</p><p>Recurrent episodes of acute pancreatitis may lead to chronic pancreatitis and insufficiency of both the exocrine and endocrine pancreatic functions. The exocrine pancreas produces digestive enzymes, the absence of which leads to malnutrition (eg, cachexia, muscle wasting), weight loss, vitamin deficiencies, and frequent, loose, foul-smelling, sometimes oily stools (steatorrhea). The endocrine pancreas produces insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin. The loss of endogenous insulin leads to the development of diabetes mellitus with symptoms including polyuria, polydipsia, and polyphagia. In this scenario, pancreatic production of insulin and glucagon are decreased, while peripheral insulin response is normal. The diagnosis of chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic insufficiency is through clinical history, imaging, and biopsy. Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency is treated with oral pancreatic enzyme and fat-soluble vitamin replacement. Additionally, the development of diabetes mellitus caused by the loss of pancreatic insulin requires insulin replacement. Any modifiable inciting causes of pancreatitis (eg, gallstones, alcohol abuse, hypertriglyceridemia, or hypercalcemia) should be mitigated to delay disease progression and preserve remaining native pancreatic function.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Choice B, characterized by decreased insulin production, increased glucagon production, and normal peripheral insulin response, corresponds to the normal fasting state. Glucagon production also occurs in the pancreas and is affected in tandem with insulin deficiency in the setting of chronic pancreatitis. Choice C, characterized by increased insulin production, decreased glucagon production, and decreased peripheral insulin response, corresponds to type 2 diabetes mellitus, wherein a chronic hyperinsulinemic state occurs in response to poor peripheral tissue response to insulin. Choice D, characterized by increased insulin production, increased glucagon production, and decreased peripheral insulin response, is incorrect. While both insulin and glucagon concentrations may be transiently increased in the setting of acute pancreatitis, this does not commonly occur in chronic pancreatitis, which is also not associated with decreased peripheral insulin response. Choice E, characterized by increased insulin production, normal glucagon production, and decreased peripheral insulin response, may occur in the setting of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Choice F, characterized by normal insulin production, normal glucagon production, and decreased peripheral insulin response, may occur in the early stages of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Peripheral insulin response is not normally affected in the setting of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with nephrotic syndrome classically present with dependent edema in the buttocks, lower back, and legs, foamy or dark-colored urine, hypoalbuminemia, hyperlipidemia, and proteinuria. Loss of antithrombin-3 in the urine leads to hypercoagulability and a pro-thrombotic state.</p><p>Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by edema secondary to excessive proteinuria resulting in hypoalbuminemia and diminished intravascular oncotic pressure. This occurs because of the loss of normal size and charge filtration of the glomerular capillary and Bowman capsule interface. Causes of nephrotic syndrome include minimal change disease, membranous nephropathy, and focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and can be idiopathic or triggered by a recent infection or immune stimulus. Patients classically present with dependent edema in the buttocks, lower back, and legs, foamy or dark-colored urine, hypoalbuminemia, hyperlipidemia, and proteinuria. Complications include hypercoagulability caused by the urinary loss of proteins such as antithrombin-3, which functions to inhibit coagulation factors II, VII, IX, X, XI, and XII. Deficiency of antithrombin-3 leads to hypercoagulability and would put this patient at high risk for thrombotic conditions such as deep venous thrombosis. Patients with nephrotic syndrome are also at an increased risk for infection because of the urinary loss of gamma globulins.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Hypothyroidism (Choice B) can present with peripheral edema, but often presents with a constellation of symptoms including fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, hyporeflexia, and dry, cool skin.</p><p>Nephrolithiasis (Choice C) can occur in settings of increased formation of calcium oxalate, uric acid, cystine, or struvite crystals. Cystine crystals occur in patients with a hereditary defect of the renal proximal convoluted tubule that results in the decreased reabsorption of cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine. Uric acid calculi form most commonly in patients with hyperuricemia (eg, gout) and struvite calculi occur in the setting of infections caused by urease positive microbial pathogens, such as Klebsiella or Proteus species.</p><p>Peripheral vascular disease (Choice D) occurs secondary to decreased arterial blood flow through stenotic arteries, which can present with ischemic ulcers on the lower extremities and tips of the digits, particularly the toes. Peripheral vascular disease is common among older, male smokers with a history of hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and/or hyperlipidemia.</p><p>Stasis dermatitis (Choice E) presents as erythema, itching, and hyperpigmentation of skin caused by chronic inflammation from the third spacing fluid, and it is often seen in the bilateral lower extremities in cases of heart failure or impaired venous return.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute tubular necrosis is a cause of acute oliguric renal failure that can occur after an ischemic insult to the kidneys, such as in hypovolemic or hemorrhagic shock.</p><p>Acute tubular necrosis is a cause of intrinsic acute renal failure that is most commonly the result of either ischemic or nephrotoxic insults to the kidneys. This patient presents after traumatic hemorrhagic shock with hemodynamic instability and signs of hypovolemia. This increases the risk for ischemic injury to the kidneys secondary to decreased renal blood flow, which can lead to tubular epithelial cell death and sloughing into the lumen of the tubules. This typically results in an initial oliguric phase where the patient is at increased risk for uremia, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis. Following this initial phase, the patient will then enter a polyuric, recovery phase characterized by excessive urine production with associated falls in creatinine and risk for electrolyte abnormalities, especially hypokalemia. Urinalysis typically discloses granular, muddy brown casts. Though not seen in this case, the presence of blood and protein in the urine may indicate an additional underlying pathology in trauma patients, such as rhabdomyolysis from compression necrosis of muscle caused by trauma or compartment syndrome.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Hypovolemia (Choice B) itself does not explain the patient's oliguria because the patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and blood products, and the patient's vital signs subsequently normalized.</p><p>Interstitial nephritis (Choice C) most commonly presents as a hypersensitivity reaction following exposure to certain medications such as penicillin, diuretics, and sulfonamides. It can also occur in the setting of autoimmune diseases such as systemic lupus erythematosus. It typically presents with pyuria, predominantly eosinophilic.</p><p>Transfusion reactions (Choice D) are variable and can involve febrile transfusion reactions secondary to pre-formed cytokines in the transfused blood, hemolytic transfusion reactions related to inaccurate cross-matching or administration of an incorrect blood type to the patient, or anaphylaxis which would be characterized by tachycardia, wheezing, hypotension, angioedema, itching, urticaria, nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea. The patient is not exhibiting any such signs, symptoms, or laboratory values, making a transfusion reaction less likely.</p><p>Urinary tract obstruction (Choice E) can occur in such settings as nephrolithiasis, fibrosis, infection, benign prostatic hyperplasia, or ureteral obstruction, which can result in postrenal azotemia from impaired urinary drainage. This patient is young and has no clear predisposing feature for the development of a urinary tract obstruction, making it less likely to be the cause of his oliguria.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory medications can result in an acute kidney injury caused by deficiency in prostaglandins with associated reduction in renal afferent arteriole vasodilation and renal blood flow.</p><p>Acute kidney injury, a rapid decrease in renal function, may be caused by prerenal, intrinsic, or postrenal causes. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory medications (NSAIDs) such as ibuprofen are inhibitors of cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, which have a primary role in the synthesis of prostaglandins and prostacyclins. These molecules play key roles in the modulation of pain sensation and thermoregulation, the maintenance of the gastric mucosal barrier, and the regulation of renal blood flow (RBF). If NSAID use occurs at higher doses, with more potent agents or over a prolonged time, acute kidney injury (AKI) can occur because of deficiency in prostaglandins, which would otherwise promote renal afferent arteriole vasodilation and RBF. AKI sustained in this manner is especially likely if high-risk NSAID use is accompanied by RBF-limiting pre-renal insults such as volume depletion. NSAIDs can also cause intrinsic acute renal injury through the risk for acute interstitial nephritis (AIN). AIN is characterized by declining urine output and glomerular filtration rate, rising creatinine, eosinophilic infiltrates on renal biopsy, and eosinophiluria. It is often associated with a rash, and is often triggered by medications including NSAIDs, diuretics, and antibiotics. In this case, this patient's rise in creatinine indicating AKI is likely caused by ibuprofen use, which should be discontinued.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Continue ibuprofen at the current dose and repeat the laboratory studies in 2 weeks (Choice A) and continue ibuprofen at the current dose and begin oral hydrocortisone (Choice B) would be inappropriate because ibuprofen use is likely to be the cause of this patient's AKI.</p><p>Decrease the dose of ibuprofen and begin oral prednisone (Choice C) would not adequately address this patient's NSAID-induced kidney injury, and cessation of the drug is necessary.</p><p>Switch from ibuprofen to celecoxib (Choice E) would not be appropriate because celecoxib is also an NSAID and would perpetuate, if not worsen, this patient's AKI.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bladder outlet obstruction from benign prostatic hyperplasia typically presents with urinary retention, nighttime overflow incontinence, an enlarged prostate, and a weak urinary stream.</p><p>Urinary retention, nighttime overflow incontinence, an enlarged prostate, and a weak urinary stream are characteristic of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) in older men, which can lead to bladder outlet (neck) obstruction. The best next step to relieve a bladder outlet obstruction involves catheterization to decompress the urinary bladder and prevent acute complications of prolonged bladder outlet obstruction such as bladder rupture, urothelial cell atrophy or necrosis, hydroureter, hydronephrosis, post-renal azotemia, and/or renal failure. A post-void residual volume of urine should generally be less than 50-100 mL, and significant urinary retention beyond 200-250 mL (though clinical guidelines vary) is generally an indication for placement of a urinary catheter to relieve obstruction.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Detrusor muscle hyperactivity (Choice B) defines overactive bladder and uninhibited bladder contractions. It commonly results in urge incontinence, which presents with the urge to void followed by the leakage of urine. It does not necessarily occur with a large volume of urine in the bladder.</p><p>Neurogenic bladder (Choice C) is often caused by spinal cord injuries or primary nervous system pathology (eg, multiple sclerosis) with loss of appropriate interplay between the signals from the sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic nervous system, all of which are required for appropriately coordinated bladder filling and emptying. In this patient, overflow incontinence caused by bladder outlet obstruction from benign prostatic hyperplasia is more likely. Neurogenic bladder often presents as urge or overflow incontinence, depending on the aspect of neural control which is dysregulated.</p><p>A prostatic infection (Choice D) can present with incontinence as well as dysuria and a tender, boggy prostate on examination. A urinalysis would be abnormal and show numerous white blood cells.</p><p>Stress incontinence results from urethral hypermobility (Choice E), pelvic floor weakness, or intrinsic sphincter deficiency. Risk factors include multiple vaginal deliveries, obesity, and prostate surgery. Stress incontinence is triggered by an increase in intra-abdominal pressure (eg, lifting, sneezing, Valsalva) unopposed by counterpressure from the pelvic floor or urethral sphincters, resulting in leakage of urine.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Severe bladder outlet obstruction can result in acute kidney injury. Placement of a urinary catheter is the appropriate first step in management to relieve the obstruction.</p><p>This patient's urinary retention, nighttime overflow incontinence, an enlarged prostate, and a weak urinary stream is suggestive of bladder outlet obstruction from benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). As shown in this patient, BPH can result in complications such as urinary tract infection, as evidenced by fever, dysuria, and white blood cells on urinalysis. In addition, severe bladder outlet obstruction can result in a postrenal acute kidney injury and increased serum creatinine level. The first appropriate step in management is relief of the bladder outlet obstruction by placement of a urinary catheter. The already narrowed prostatic urethra, if affected by secondary inflammation or infection, can result in near complete bladder outflow obstruction. Impaired drainage of the bladder and urethra predisposes to infection by limiting urinary washout of ascending pathogens, often enteric organisms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F.</p><p>Ultrasonography of the kidneys (Choice A) would be helpful in evaluating for severity and laterality of hydronephrosis in the setting of a urinary tract obstruction but would not be an appropriate first step, which should be focused on urgent treatment of the outlet obstruction. Similarly, CT scan of the pelvis (Choice B) can help with identifying and characterizing anatomical causes of outlet obstruction, but further diagnostic strategies should not take priority over alleviating obstruction.</p><p>Furosemide therapy (Choice C) is used in the treatment of hypervolemia, which would not be appropriate in this patient who has no signs of lower extremity edema, pulmonary edema, ascites, or other signs of volume overload. Furosemide, a diuretic, may also increase the severity of acute kidney injury in this patient by reducing volume status and decreasing renal blood flow.</p><p>Levofloxacin therapy (Choice D) would be appropriate in the management of this patient's urinary tract infection secondary to outlet obstruction. First, the outlet obstruction must be addressed.</p><p>Hemodialysis (Choice F) would be appropriate in patients with severe acute kidney injury resulting in volume overload, severe electrolyte abnormalities such as hyperkalemia, uremia, or acidosis. While this patient may require dialysis, addressing the urinary outlet obstruction first may rapidly improve renal function.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neisseria gonorrhoeae can cause vaginitis, cervicitis, PID, and a multifocal dermatitis that typically presents with tender macular and/or pustular lesions. Ceftriaxone is the antibiotic of choice for Neisseria gonorrhoeae.</p><p>Neisseria gonorrhoeae can cause vaginitis, cervicitis, and pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) in women. It can also cause a multifocal dermatitis that typically presents with tender macular and/or pustular lesions along with disseminated and migratory polyarthritis. Risk factors for PID and other sexually transmitted infections include adolescence, multiple sexual partners, and not using barrier contraception such as condoms. Vaginitis typically presents with mucopurulent discharge and pruritus. Cervicitis presents with similar discharge but also with an erythematous, friable cervix and cervical motion tenderness on examination. When infection spreads to the fallopian tubes and ovaries, PID with purulent cervical discharge, uterine and adnexal tenderness, and systemic symptoms such as fever can result. If left untreated, PID can be complicated by tubo-ovarian or intra-abdominal abscess, presenting with focal tenderness and a fluctuant mass. The antibiotic treatment of Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection involves the use of third generation cephalosporins such as ceftriaxone, as well as concurrent treatment of Chlamydia trachomatis with doxycycline.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Azithromycin (Choice A) would treat an infection from Chlamydia trachomatis or trichomoniasis, as well as bacterial vaginosis. However, it does not provide appropriate antimicrobial coverage for Neisseria gonorrhoeae.</p><p>Nafcillin (Choice C) is used to treat gram-positive organisms, especially methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus. It is not the antibiotic of choice to treat Neisseria gonorrhoeae.</p><p>Penicillin (Choice D) is the treatment of choice for syphilis, which is a sexually transmitted disease from the spirochete Treponema pallidum that can present in multiple stages. Primarily syphilis presents with a painless chancre (ulcer) of the genitalia. Secondary syphilis can present with macular, not pustular lesions of the hands and feet. Syphilis would not typically cause vaginal discharge.</p><p>Vancomycin (Choice E) would not appropriately cover the causative organisms of vaginitis. It is effective in the treatment of resistant organisms such as methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus and ampicillin-resistant Enterococcus spp. It does not have significant activity against Neisseria gonorrhoeae.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Chronic autoimmune thyroiditis is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-replete parts of the world. The disease is more common in women than men, and patients with a family history of other autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes mellitus or primary adrenal insufficiency are at a higher risk for hypothyroidism.</p><p>This patient's family history of a brother with type 1 diabetes mellitus is the most important historical clue because it suggests an increased risk for familial autoimmune disease. While there are many varieties of thyroid disease that can lead to hypothyroidism, the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-replete parts of the world is Hashimoto thyroiditis, also known as chronic autoimmune thyroiditis. Lymphocytic infiltration of the thyroid gland leads to cytotoxic destruction of the gland and subsequent fibrosis, which results in hypothyroidism. Patients may either have a goiter or a normal size thyroid gland. The diagnosis is made by measuring the thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) concentration, which will be increased, and the free thyroxine concentration (FT ,), which will be decreased. This pattern indicates adequate secretion of TSH from the pituitary gland but inadequate production of thyroxine in the thyroid gland. In some cases, patients may have antibodies against thyroid peroxidase or thyroglobulin, although these are not required for diagnosis. As this is an autoimmune disease, patients with a family history of other autoimmune disorders such as type 1 diabetes mellitus and primary adrenal insufficiency are at greater risk for developing chronic autoimmune thyroiditis. Treatment is with thyroid hormone replacement in the form of levothyroxine.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, and F.</p><p>Alcohol consumption (Choice A) does not increase the risk for developing hypothyroidism.</p><p>Childhood illness (Choice B), including a case of meningitis, is not particularly helpful in this situation. While meningitis may increase the risk for hearing loss and seizure disorders, it is not associated with the development of hypothyroidism.</p><p>Current medications (Choice C), which include divalproex, oral contraceptive pills, and a multivitamin are not associated with an increased risk for hypothyroidism. While divalproex may rarely cause off-target endocrine effects such as amenorrhea, it is not directly associated with hypothyroidism.</p><p>History of motor vehicle collision (Choice E) leading to hypopituitarism after traumatic brain injury can cause central hypothyroidism, but this presents with a decreased TSH and decreased FT4. Additionally, it would be unusual for a patient to exhibit isolated central hypothyroidism without deficiencies in other pituitary hormones.</p><p>Illicit drug use (Choice F) can occasionally have endocrine complications. For example, chronic opiate use can cause secondary hypogonadism in men, but marijuana is not known to be associated with hypothyroidism.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypoparathyroidism may occur secondary to autoimmune destruction of the parathyroid glands or from surgical removal during thyroidectomy. Decreased PTH concentrations lead to hypocalcemia and inadequate conversion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol. Symptoms of hypocalcemia may include perioral paresthesia, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, prolonged QT interval, Chvostek sign, and Trousseau sign. Treatment is with calcium and calcitriol supplements.</p><p>Deficiency of parathyroid hormone (PTH), also known as hypoparathyroidism, likely explains this patient's symptomatic hypocalcemia. PTH is a peptide hormone that increases osteoclast activity and bone resorption to maintain calcium homeostasis. It is produced by the four parathyroid glands, which are located on the posterior aspect of the thyroid gland. Primary hypoparathyroidism can occur following the unintentional resection of the parathyroid glands during thyroidectomy or may be caused by autoimmune destruction of the parathyroid glands. Primary hypoparathyroidism results in decreased concentrations of PTH, which leads to hypocalcemia secondary to inadequate bone resorption, hyperphosphatemia, and decreased concentrations of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol) because PTH stimulates renal conversion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to its active form. Decreased concentrations of calcitriol substantially worsen hypocalcemia as calcitriol directly stimulates intestinal calcium absorption. Signs and symptoms of severe hypocalcemia may include perioral paresthesia, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, and prolonged QT interval. The Chvostek sign, in which tapping of the facial nerve leads to spasm of the facial muscles, and Trousseau sign, in which an inflated blood pressure cuff occluding the brachial artery causes carpopedal spasm, are two physical examination signs that may indicate hypocalcemia. Treatment of primary hypoparathyroidism is with calcium and calcitriol supplementation to achieve a low-normal calcium level.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, and F.</p><p>Autonomous secretion of aldosterone (Choice A), known as primary hyperaldosteronism, usually results in hypokalemia, alkalosis, and hypertension. This patient's serum potassium is normal, and her blood pressure is also within a normal range.</p><p>Autonomous secretion of parathyroid hormone (Choice B) is known as primary hyperparathyroidism and results in hypercalcemia, not hypocalcemia. Symptoms of hypercalcemia may include weakness, constipation, confusion, depression, diffuse bone pain, nephrolithiasis, and polyuria.</p><p>Deficiency of vitamin B12 (cobalamin) (Choice D) can lead to paresthesia and reduced proprioception because of subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal and lateral columns of the spinal cord. Patients present with impaired gait, an abnormal Romberg test, and often with macrocytic anemia.</p><p>Excess secretion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D (Choice E) is not a well-recognized clinical syndrome, although hypersecretion of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, a highly active form of vitamin D, is occasionally seen in patients with sarcoidosis or other granulomatous disorders. Vitamin D excess can lead to hypercalcemia, not hypocalcemia.</p><p>Excess secretion of magnesium (Choice F) in the urine leading to hypomagnesemia is frequently seen in inherited diseases of the kidney including juxtaglomerular cell hyperplasia and Gitelman syndrome, but it typically presents with hypokalemia as well.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Increased prolactin concentrations may be seen in patients who take dopamine antagonists such as antipsychotics, but markedly increased concentrations, especially when seen in combination with amenorrhea and galactorrhea, are more likely to represent autonomous secretion of prolactin from an anterior pituitary adenoma. The first step in evaluation should be an MRI of the pituitary gland.</p><p>MRI of the pituitary gland is the most appropriate next step in evaluation of this young woman with amenorrhea, galactorrhea, and an increased serum concentration of prolactin. Prolactin is a hormone that is produced and secreted by the anterior pituitary gland. Normal increases in serum prolactin occur during pregnancy and immediately following delivery when infants begin to breast feed, but concentrations return to normal several weeks after delivery even as lactation continues. Pathologic increases in prolactin concentration are commonly the result of pituitary adenomas resulting in hypersecretion of prolactin from lactotroph cells. While dopamine antagonists such as antipsychotic medications or selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors may lead to mild increases of prolactin concentration, they do not approach the concentrations seen in pituitary adenomas. Prolactin inhibits the release of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) and thereby reduces the concentrations of follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone, which leads to amenorrhea and infertility. Prolactin also acts directly on mammary glands in the breast and induces lactation, although galactorrhea is less common in premenopausal women. After medications are excluded as the potential cause, patients should undergo MRI of the pituitary gland to assess for the presence of a mass. In most cases, the first-line treatment is with cabergoline, a dopamine agonist, which decreases secretion of prolactin. This medication is routinely used for women who desire pregnancy, as in this case, although bromocriptine is a reasonable alternative. In rare circumstances, transsphenoidal resection may be required.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Cytologic examination of breast secretions (Choice A) is incorrect. While unilateral galactorrhea may be concerning for breast cancer in some instances, the constellation of symptoms in this patient, including bilateral galactorrhea and amenorrhea, are more consistent with a prolactinoma.</p><p>Measurement of serum estradiol concentration (Choice B) is not necessary. Concentrations fluctuate throughout the menstrual cycle and during menopause, but this patient's increased prolactin concentration indicates a more likely cause of amenorrhea. Measurement of serum follicle-stimulating (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH) concentrations (Choice C) is unlikely to be helpful in this case. Prolactin suppresses the secretion of GnRH and will thereby lead to suppression of both FSH and LH.</p><p>Mammography (Choice D) is used to screen for breast cancer in women over the age of 40 years or to further evaluate palpable masses detected on breast examination. This patient's galactorrhea is more likely the result of a prolactinoma and is unlikely to represent a primary breast pathology.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Toxic shock syndrome is caused by toxic shock syndrome toxin, which is produced by Staphylococcus aureus and causes a full body erythematous eruption. Patients also classically exhibit fever, vital sign instability, and signs of end organ dysfunction related to the underlying systemic shock. Treatment of the inciting staphylococcal infection and supportive care are warranted.</p><p>This patient presents with signs and symptoms of toxic shock syndrome. Toxic shock syndrome toxin (TSST-1), produced by Staphylococcus aureus, causes a cytokine storm, which causes dysfunction of multiple organs and can lead to distributive shock and death. Patients typically present with fever, hypotension, diffuse erythematous rash, desquamation, and clinical and laboratory evidence of end organ dysfunction such as altered mental status, myalgia, thrombocytopenia, azotemia, and transaminitis. Toxic shock may be rapidly fatal, even with appropriate treatment. Treatment includes the administration of appropriate antibiotics, aggressive hemodynamic support with intravenous fluids, and support of end organ failure. Source control, such as a pelvic examination to remove a retained tampon, is important to limit further release of toxin.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Oral prednisone (Choice A) is a glucocorticoid used in a variety of systemic inflammatory conditions (eg, severe persistent asthma) but would be ineffective for the treatment of Staphylococcus aureus.</p><p>Intravenous dopamine (Choice B) is a vasopressor that could be used if shock persists despite appropriate fluid resuscitation. This patient should first receive fluid resuscitation intravenously and be monitored for response. In addition, dopamine is not typically a first-line agent for vasoactive support because it is proarrhythmic. Norepinephrine is preferred as the initial pressor in the setting of septic shock.</p><p>Intravenous metronidazole (Choice D) and penicillin (Choice E) are inappropriate antibiotics and would not appropriately treat a Staphylococcus aureus infection. Metronidazole is an antibiotic used in the management of anaerobic infections, notably Clostridium difficile and Giardia lamblia. Penicillin is an antibiotic used to treat various gram-positive and spirochete infections. Staphylococcus aureus is resistant to most penicillins because of its penicillinase enzyme that cleaves the β-lactam ring of the penicillin molecule.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Temporal arteritis is a large-vessel vasculitis that often presents with headache, temporal tenderness, jaw claudication, and fever. A potential acute complication is ischemic optic neuropathy, which can lead to monocular blindness.</p><p>Granulomatous arteritis in cervical or temporal arteries, also referred to as temporal or giant cell arteritis is a vasculitis that affects the large vessels such as the aorta, aortic arch vessels, the temporal branch of the external carotid, and vertebral arteries, among others. It is characterized by headache, temporal tenderness, jaw claudication, and fever. A potential complication includes monocular blindness from ischemic optic neuropathy. This occurs secondary to inflammation and resultant thrombosis of the central retinal artery and/or posterior ciliary arteries. Because of this potential acute complication, this condition requires the urgent administration of glucocorticoids, ophthalmologic evaluation, and temporal artery biopsy for diagnosis. Administration of steroids does not generally affect biopsy results if biopsy occurs within 1 to 2 weeks.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Dendritic corneal ulceration (Choice A) can be seen in herpes simplex virus keratitis, which can cause conjunctivitis and blepharoconjunctivitis. It is not related to temporal arteritis.</p><p>Hyperviscosity of the retinal veins (Choice B) can lead to central retinal vein occlusion, which presents with floaters along with blurry or distorted vision. Patients with high blood pressure, diabetes mellitus, and hyperviscosity syndromes (eg, myeloma, leukemia) are at a higher risk for central retinal vein occlusion.</p><p>Scleromalacia perforans (Choice D) is a rare disorder involving painless autoimmune damage of episcleral and scleral vessels. It causes scleral thinning and does not cause vision loss.</p><p>Uveitis (Choice E) can be a complication of ankylosing spondylitis, which is a rheumatologic inflammatory condition involving the vertebrae that presents with subacute to chronic back pain and a limited range of motion. It presents with injected conjunctiva, eye pain, and visual disturbance. Cells and flare are seen in the anterior chamber on slit lamp examination.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Raynaud phenomenon refers to vasospasm that causes intermittent loss of blood flow to the distal extremities in response to cold, stress, or emotional upset. It may be idiopathic or associated with mixed connected tissue disease, systemic lupus erythematosus, or CREST syndrome.</p><p>Vasospasm causes the intermittent loss of blood flow to the distal extremities in Raynaud phenomenon, resulting in sequential white, blue, and red discoloration of the affected areas of skin. The vasospasm associated with Raynaud phenomenon can be triggered and exacerbated by cold temperature, stress, or emotional upset. Activities such as cigarette smoking, injury, trauma, or chemical exposure can put patients at a higher risk for Raynaud phenomenon. Raynaud phenomenon may be idiopathic or associated with mixed connected tissue disease, systemic lupus erythematosus, or CREST syndrome. When severe or prolonged, ischemic injury can result in digital ulcerations. Dressing warmly in cold weather can help prevent Raynaud phenomenon by decreasing cold exposure.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Doxazosin (Choice B) is an α-adrenergic blocker that can help treat hypertension, post-traumatic stress disorder, and urinary retention from benign prostatic hyperplasia. It has not been conclusively shown to improve symptoms of Raynaud phenomenon.</p><p>Prednisone therapy (Choice C) is a glucocorticoid used in a variety of systemic inflammatory conditions such as scleroderma, which can be associated with Raynaud phenomenon, but it would not directly address the mechanism of vasospasm.</p><p>Propranolol therapy (Choice D) is a non-selective β-adrenergic blocker that is used in the treatment of hypertension, hyperthyroidism, anxiety disorders, and portal hypertension. It is not generally employed in the treatment of Raynaud phenomenon.</p><p>Sympathectomy (Choice E) refers to the removal of a sympathetic nerve root, and is often used in hyperhidrosis, reflex sympathetic dystrophy, and Raynaud disease. It is an invasive surgery and would be reserved for refractory, treatment-resistant, severe cases.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Heat stroke occurs when the body is exposed to high environmental temperature and humidity, and it is characterized by hyperthermia, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, and multi-organ system dysfunction that includes neurologic dysfunction. Heat stroke may be complicated by shock, rhabdomyolysis, and disseminated intravascular coagulation.</p><p>Heat stroke is characterized by an increased body temperature that occurs when the body is exposed to high environmental temperatures and high humidity. It commonly happens in young athletes or in elderly patients with impaired thermoregulatory capacity. Excessive heat exposure and increased core body temperature lead to cardiovascular and neurologic dysfunction with secondary tachycardia, tachypnea, and hypotension. Vasodilation of superficial blood vessels may lead to shunting of blood with resultant multi-organ system dysfunction, including neurologic impairment, hepatic and renal ischemia, rhabdomyolysis, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Treatment includes cooling of the patient with fans or ice water, continuous core temperature monitoring, and support of the patient's airway, breathing, and circulation. A patient must demonstrate neurologic symptoms to diagnose heat stroke, whereas heat exhaustion presents similarly but with an intact neurologic examination. In this example, the patient is a young manual laborer who works outside in the sun; his use of an antihistamine (especially if it also has anticholinergic action) may also impair his ability to thermoregulate through sweat and cutaneous vasodilation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Malignant hyperthermia (Choice B) is characterized by rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability, often in the operative or post-operative setting following the administration of volatile inhaled anesthetics, such as halothane or isoflurane, or paralytics such as succinylcholine.</p><p>Myocardial ischemia (Choice C) presents with chest pain, shortness of breath, and ST-segment elevation or depression or T-wave inversion on ECG. Hyperthermia and environmental heat exposure suggest an alternate diagnosis.</p><p>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (Choice D) occurs secondary to certain classes of medications, such as antipsychotics, and is characterized by hyperthermia, muscle rigidity, and autonomic instability. This patient's recent environmental exposure to heat is not consistent with neuroleptic malignant syndrome.</p><p>Thyroid storm (Choice E) is a serious potential complication of hyperthyroidism following an acute stress such as trauma or infection. Characteristic presenting symptoms of thyroid storm include fever, diarrhea, tachyarrhythmia, altered mental status (eg, delirium, agitation), and potential coma or death if not emergently treated. Environmental heat exposure makes heat stroke more likely.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Anaphylaxis should be suspected in patients who present with acute-onset bronchoconstriction and evidence of distributive shock. Immediate treatment with intramuscular epinephrine is critical and generally results in rapid improvement in symptoms. Additional interventions should focus on maintaining adequate blood pressure and ensuring patency of the airway.</p><p>Administration of epinephrine is the most appropriate immediate step in management for this patient with presumed anaphylaxis. Anaphylaxis should be suspected given tachycardia, hypotension, hypoxia, and wheezing, which is most likely indicative of bronchospasm. Anaphylaxis is a Type 1 hypersensitivity reaction that develops following exposure to an allergen. It is caused by widespread IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation and is characterized by histaminergic responses within multiple organ systems leading to flushing, angioedema, bronchospasm, nausea, vomiting, abdominal discomfort, and vasodilation, which can lead to airway obstruction and circulatory collapse. If not managed promptly, death can occur from hypoxia and/or distributive shock. The diagnosis is made clinically, and the first treatment that should be given is intramuscular epinephrine. Adjunctive management may include inhaled β-adrenergic agonists, glucocorticoids, histamine blockers, fluid resuscitation, and potentially invasive airway or vasopressor support. Patients who present with signs and symptoms of anaphylaxis while working outdoors may have experienced bee envenomation. Regardless of the cause, treatment is the same. At the time of discharge, all patients should be provided with an epinephrine autoinjector and training on how to use it.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, F, and G.</p><p>Arterial blood gas analysis (Choice A) provides information about a patient's acid-base status and oxygenation. In this case, the patient has clear evidence of hypoxia and should be treated immediately with therapies aimed at reversing anaphylaxis. Arterial blood gas analysis is not necessary and would only cause unnecessary delays in treatment.</p><p>Determination of eosinophil count (Choice B) is not necessary. Hypereosinophilia is a nonspecific finding that is frequently present in patients with asthma and atopic disorders. This patient is unstable and requires immediate treatment.</p><p>Determination of peak expiratory flow rate (Choice C) is useful for assessing patients with suspected asthma exacerbations because measurement in the emergency department can be compared to the patient's baseline to determine the severity of bronchospasm. It is not useful for patients who have clear evidence of life-threatening bronchospasm or laryngospasm as evidenced by an inability to talk.</p><p>Measurement of plasma histamine concentration (Choice D) can support the diagnosis of anaphylaxis, but measurement should never delay emergent treatment of this life-threatening condition.</p><p>Toxicology screen (Choice E) is indicated for patients who are suspected of having overdosed. This patient's presentation is more consistent with anaphylaxis and should be treated as such without delay.</p><p>Administration of corticosteroids (Choice F) and diphenhydramine (Choice G) is appropriate only after the administration of epinephrine. These are adjunct medications that do not immediately reverse the life-threatening symptoms of anaphylaxis.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Metabolic acidosis is often caused by an inadequate oxygen supply to end organs, which causes them to transition from aerobic metabolism to anaerobic metabolism with the consequent production of lactic acid. This can occur in cardiogenic shock, caused by a reduction in cardiac output secondary to infarcted or infected cardiac muscle. It can also occur in other forms of shock, such as hypovolemic or septic, also caused by the inadequate supply of oxygen to the body's tissues.</p><p>Following a myocardial infarction, hypotension, altered mental status, and hypoperfused (cool, clammy) skin is most likely caused by cardiogenic shock. Cardiogenic shock is caused by a decrease in cardiac output, often caused by infarcted or infected cardiac muscle. This leads to vasoconstriction of peripheral vessels to maintain blood pressure and inadequate perfusion of end organs. As organs begin to receive an insufficient supply of oxygen, they transition from aerobic metabolism to anaerobic metabolism, which leads to the production of lactic acid. This lactic acid decreases the pH of the blood, leading to a metabolic acidosis. This is often worsened in cardiogenic shock because the liver is unable to adequately metabolize lactate in the setting of decreased perfusion. Therefore, this patient likely has a metabolic acidosis caused by the production of lactic acid.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Hypoxemia with normal pH (Choice A) can be seen with primarily lung pathologies, such as an asthma or COPD exacerbation. However, prolonged hypoxemia often leads to a metabolic acidosis caused by an inadequate supply of oxygen to tissues, causing transition to anaerobic metabolism with consequent lactate production. This patient is more likely to have a metabolic acidosis related to decreased cardiac output than persistent hypoxemia with a normal pH.</p><p>Metabolic alkalosis (Choice C) is often associated with the loss of acid, often from the gastrointestinal tract. For example, persistent emesis can cause metabolic alkalosis, as can the use of diuretics. This patient has no clear source of a loss of hydrogens ions, making metabolic alkalosis unlikely.</p><p>Respiratory acidosis (Choice D) can be acute or chronic. Acute respiratory acidosis is often caused by hypoventilation, such as in the case of opioid-induced respiratory depression. Chronic, compensated respiratory acidosis is more commonly seen in patients with obstructive lung disease or obesity hypoventilation syndrome. This patient's cardiogenic shock would not lead to a respiratory acidosis.</p><p>Respiratory alkalosis (Choice E) occurs in the setting of hyperventilation, such as in the case of pain or anxiety. It can also be caused by hyperventilation caused by pulmonary embolism, fever, pregnancy, a high-altitude environment, and aspirin overdose. This patient's inadequate organ perfusion is more likely to cause a metabolic acidosis than a respiratory alkalosis.</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Central line-associated infections are often caused by skin flora that are introduced into the bloodstream during insertion of the line or during its handling. They present with fever, erythema, tenderness, and leukocytosis, with the most common causal organisms including skin flora, such as Staphylococcal species. Treatment includes intravenous antibiotics and line removal.</p><p>Bacteremia commonly presents with fevers, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, and leukocytosis, and can also be associated with altered mental status and end organ damage if it proceeds to cause septic shock. While it can be caused by a variety of infections, such as pneumonia or pyelonephritis, it is most likely caused by this patient's central line given its erythematous and tender insertion site. Central line catheters are most often placed for the administration of vasopressors in critically ill patients, as an alternative for venous access in patients with difficult to obtain peripheral access, and for the long-term administration of medications, such as antibiotics or chemotherapy. Unfortunately, central line-associated bloodstream infections are a relatively common complication. Bacteria, most often skin flora such as staphylococcal species, can be introduced into the bloodstream and lead to bacteremia, especially in patients who are immunocompromised, such as this patient undergoing chemotherapy. Therefore, the most likely causal organism in this patient is Staphylococcus epidermidis, a common cutaneous organism that can translocate and seed discontinuities in skin such as line sites. Treatment includes intravenous antibiotics and removal of the line.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Candida albicans (Choice A) is a fungal organism that most commonly causes oropharyngeal candidiasis, esophagitis, and vulvovaginitis. Candidemia, or the invasion of Candida into the blood, is rare and often only seen in immunocompromised patients, with other risk factors including admission to the ICU, central line access, and the administration of total parenteral nutrition. However, candidemia often leads to severe sepsis with associated skin and ophthalmologic lesions, which this patient does not have.</p><p>Clostridium perfringens (Choice B) is a common pathogen in necrotizing skin infections, which present with fever, pain out of proportion to examination, erythema, ecchymosis, edema, and bullae, often in immunocompromised or diabetic patients. It progresses rapidly over hours to days and requires emergent surgical debridement as it can lead to septic shock and death. This patient's central line-associated bloodstream infection is more likely caused by the introduction of skin flora into the blood, such as Staphylococcus epidermidis.</p><p>Escherichia coli (Choice C) is a gram-negative rod that frequently causes urinary tract infections, which can be a source of infection, bacteremia, and sepsis, particularly in immunocompromised patients. However, urinary tract infections often present with dysuria, urgency, frequency, and hematuria, rather than skin erythema and tenderness as seen in this patient.</p><p>Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Choice D) is a gram-negative rod that most commonly causes hospital-acquired or healthcare-associated infections, such as pneumonia. It is less commonly a source of central line-associated infections, making Staphylococcal epidermidis a more likely causal organism of this patient's infection.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Sensitivity refers to the ability of a test to correctly identify those with the disease, whereas specificity refers to the ability of a test to correctly identify those without the disease. A highly sensitive test is useful for screening, whereas a highly specific test is useful for confirming the diagnosis.</p><p>Sensitivity and specificity are important concepts when determining which diagnostic tests to utilize and how to interpret them. Sensitivity refers to the ability of a test to correctly identify those with the disease, whereas specificity refers to the ability of a test to correctly identify those without the disease. In a patient population with low prevalence of a disease, a negative result on a highly sensitive test can effectively rule out the disease because there is a low rate of false negative tests. However, a positive result on a sensitive test must be verified by a highly specific test, which would have a decreased risk for false positive results. Therefore, sensitive tests are often used for screening, as in this patient, but must be verified by a more specific test. Therefore, a test with 99% specificity would be most likely to confirm or refute this patient's diagnosis. Specificity is calculated as true negatives divided by the sum of true negatives and false positives. Therefore, a test with a 99% specificity would have only one false positive out of 100 administered tests. Positive tests would therefore be more likely to be true positives.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, and C.</p><p>While tests A, B, and C are all highly sensitive, a diagnosis is more appropriately confirmed by a test with high specificity because it can more reliably determine who does not have the disease. Therefore, a positive result on a highly specific test is more likely to be indicative of disease.</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Interpreting the results of a scientific study should be done with careful consideration of methodological flaws and study weaknesses, such as confounding variables, inappropriate selection of case or control groups, inappropriate statistical analysis, and biases. Case and control groups should be meticulously chosen in a way that allows minimization of confounding variables that may alter the results of a study.</p><p>A variety of methodological flaws or study weaknesses can limit the application of study results to a general population. These flaws include confounding variables, inappropriate selection of case or control groups, inappropriate analysis of data, and biases, such as sampling bias and observation bias. In designing a case control study to determine relationships between exposures and outcomes, it is important to choose a case group and a control group that are matched to eliminate or avoid confounding to strengthen conclusions that differences observed are caused by the variables being studied. For example, in this scenario, choosing a control population that consists of patients admitted to the hospital with acute injuries likely interferes with the interpreted results of the studies that the regular use of alcohol reduced the risk for colon cancer. This is because regular alcohol use can lead to acute injuries during intoxication, and thus the control group may have a higher baseline use of alcohol compared to the case group. This has likely led to the possible inappropriate conclusion in this study that regular use of alcohol reduced the risk for colon cancer. Therefore, the most important reason to be concerned about the authors' conclusion of this study is the inappropriate control group.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, and D.</p><p>Confounding by age (Choice A) was not present in this study because the control and case group were matched by age, which would eliminate age as a confounding variable.</p><p>Inappropriate case group (Choice B) is not correct. As the authors were interested in determining the effects of regular alcohol use on colon cancer, choosing a group of patients with colon cancer is an appropriate case group for this study. This group can then be interviewed about their use of alcohol, which can be compared to the use of alcohol in an appropriate control group to determine whether regular alcohol use is linked to the development of colon cancer.</p><p>Lack of incidence rates for alcohol users and nonusers (Choice D) is not the most concerning aspect of this study. Incidence rates would be more important in evaluating the risk for colon cancer development in alcohol users and nonusers in a prospective cohort study. The results of this study are more concerning because of the lack of an appropriate control group.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hospice care is a multidisciplinary healthcare model that aims to provide symptom management, emotional and spiritual support, and access to a variety of health care providers. The purpose of hospice care is to alleviate pain and suffering at the end of a patient's life by minimizing hospital admissions, as well as diagnostic and invasive procedures. Medications provided during hospice care are often titrated to patient symptoms, with appropriate pain control being an important tenet.</p><p>Hospice care is a form of health care that aims to provide comfort and palliation to terminally ill patients in their last stages of life. As a terminally ill patient approaches the end of life, they may decide to focus on the quality of remaining life rather than on increasing the quantity of time remaining. Hospice is a multidisciplinary healthcare model that includes physicians, nurses, social workers, and chaplains and aids to eliminate suffering and provide spiritual and emotional support to patients and their family. Goals often include treating symptoms, such as pain and anxiety, and providing a patient as much time at home as possible by avoiding further hospital admissions. This patient has terminal heart failure associated with arrhythmias, as well as hemiparesis, and has opted to enroll in hospice care. Therefore, care should be taken to eliminate the symptoms related to this patient's chronic illnesses by discussing the patient's pain control with the hospice staff, rather than admitting him to the hospital again.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Spiritual counseling (Choice B) and psychiatric assessment (Choice C) are services provided by hospice care. However, neither would improve this patient's back pain to prevent another hospital admission. Titrating this patient's pain medications would be the more appropriate next step in management.</p><p>Admission to the hospital (Choice D) would not be in line with this patient's goals. It would be more appropriate to try to manage his pain as an outpatient prior to admitting to the hospital, which would limit his remaining time at home.</p><p>Echocardiography and feeding tube placement (Choice E) would be inappropriate in this patient. Transitioning to hospice care indicates a transition in care from focusing on diagnostic and invasive procedures to prioritizing comfort and symptom management. Further intervention on this patient would not likely prolong this patient's life and would only add to his pain and suffering.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Medical errors are common in healthcare. They range from active failures by providers to latent conditions of the environment, but most commonly are caused by an interplay of individual and systemic factors. When these errors are prevented from reaching the patient, they are referred to as near miss events. Errors that reach the patient are adverse events if they cause temporary harm or sentinel events if they lead to permanent disability or death.</p><p>Medical errors are common in healthcare and are often the result of an interplay of individual and systemic factors. They range from medication administration errors to failures of equipment, among a variety of others. These errors or mistakes may or may not reach the patient. Adverse events occur from medical errors that reach the patient and cause unintended harm, whereas sentinel events are an extreme form of these events that lead to permanent disability or death. However, many safeguards are put into place to prevent errors from reaching patients. For example, as in this case, the medication ordered by the physician is checked by the pharmacist prior to being dispensed, and then it would be further checked by the bedside nurse before it is delivered to the patient. These medical errors that do not reach the patient are referred to as near miss events.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Active failure (Choice A) is a medical error that is caused by a human error such as negligence, misinformation, incompetence, or a technical mistake, and directly causes the patient harm. This error was likely caused by an honest mistake in entering the medication and was realized by the pharmacist before reaching the patient, making it a near miss event rather than an active failure.</p><p>Adverse event (Choice B) is a medical error that reaches the patient and causes harm, such as giving the wrong medication to a patient. If this error had not been recognized by the pharmacist, and the medication had been administered to the patient, it may have led to hypotension in the patient, which would qualify as an adverse event.</p><p>Latent conditions (Choice C) are systemic conditions that can lead to medical mistakes and patient harm. They are often caused by the organization or design of a system. This error was caused by a mistake by the provider but was prevented from reaching the patient by the pharmacist, making it more consistent with a near miss.</p><p>Sentinel event (Choice E) is a medical error that reaches a patient and leads to permanent disability or death. Examples would include performing an operation on the wrong patient or wrong site or administering a medication to which a patient was known to be allergic, leading to anaphylaxis and/or death. This error did not reach the patient, and thus would be considered a near miss event.</p>"};
